<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>The author was supported by NSA grant H98230-08-1-0059 while
preparing this article.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dan Edidin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>University of Missouri
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:edidind@missouri.edu">edidind@missouri.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In this chapter we give a categorical definition of the integral
cohomology ring of
a stack. For quotient stacks <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> the categorical cohomology ring may be
identified with the equivariant cohomology <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Identifying
the stack cohomology ring with equivariant cohomology allows us to
prove that the cohomology ring of a quotient Deligne-Mumford stack is
rationally isomorphic to the cohomology ring of its coarse moduli
space. The theory is presented with a focus on the stacks <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth and stable curves respectively.</p>
  
</div>
