<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The study of the cohomology of the moduli space of
curves has been a very rich research area for the last 30 years.
Contributions have been made to the field from a remarkably broad
range of researchers: algebraic geometers, topologists, mathematical
physicists, hyperbolic geometers, etc. The goal of this article is to
give an introduction to some of the foundational issues related to
studying the cohomology of the moduli space from the algebro-geometric
point of view.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In algebraic geometry the main difficulty in studying the cohomology
of the moduli space is that the moduli space is not really a space but a
<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stack</span>. As a result, care is required in determining what the
cohomology ring of the moduli space should mean. In the literature
this difficulty is often dealt with by arguing that the stack of
curves is an orbifold. Associated to an orbifold is an underlying
rational homology manifold. The rational cohomology ring of the
stack is then defined to be the cohomology ring of this underlying homology
manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are two difficulties with this perspective. First, it can
somewhat confusing to sort out the technicalities of intersection
theory on orbifolds, and second, one abandons hope of obtaining a good
theory with integer coefficients. In this article we will circumvent
these difficulties by utilizing the categorical nature of stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a very natural functorial definition for the
cohomology of a stack and explain how for quotient stacks <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>,
(like <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>) our functorial cohomology can be identified with
equivariant cohomology of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Because our techniques are algebraic we
are also able to define the integral Chow ring of a stack.
On <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> the tautological classes all naturally live in
the functorial cohomology ring. In addition, if <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
smooth quotient stack then
functorial group <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the Picard group
of the “moduli problem” defined earlier by Mumford in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Using techniques from equivariant cohomology we show that for smooth
quotient stacks such as
<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the functorial cohomology groups are rationally isomorphic
to the cohomology groups of the underlying coarse moduli space. A
similar results holds for Chow groups. This isomorphism defines an
intersection product on the rational Chow groups of the projective, but
singular, moduli scheme of curves <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To give a description of the cohomology of the stack of curves we
obviously must consider a more basic question. <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What is a stack?</span>
In order to keep this chapter self-contained but still of a reasonable
length we will give a very brief introduction to theory of
Deligne-Mumford stacks via a series of examples. Most of our
discussion will focus on quotient stacks, because the geometry of
quotient stacks is, in essence, equivariant geometry of ordinary
schemes. Most stacks that naturally arise in algebraic geometry, such
as <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are in fact quotient stacks. For a further
introduction to Deligne-Mumford
stacks the reader is encouraged to look at Section 4
of Deligne and Mumford’s paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>]</cite> as well as Section 7 of
Vistoli’s paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vis1</a>]</cite>. The author’s paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edi</a>]</cite> gives
an introduction to Deligne-Mumford stacks from the perspective of the
moduli space of curves. The book by Laumon and Moret-Bailly
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">LMB</a>]</cite> is the most comprehensive (and most technical) treatise
on the theory of algebraic stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The Chapter is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Categories fibred in groupoids (CFGs) ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we
define and give examples of <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">categories fibred in groupoids</span> (CFGs). Our
main focus is on <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quotient CFGs</span> - that is CFGs arising from actions of linear algebraic groups on schemes.
In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we define the cohomology and Chow rings of a
CFG and prove that, for quotient CFGs, these rings can be identified
with equivariant cohomology and Chow rings respectively. In Sections
<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> we define
Deligne-Mumford stacks and their coarse moduli spaces. Finally in
Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> we explain the relationship between the
cohomology ring of a Deligne-Mumford stack and its coarse moduli
space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgments:</span> The author is grateful to Damiano Fulghesu and to the referee for a number of helpful comments on earlier versions of this Chapter. The author also thanks Andrew Kresch for sending him a copy of his preprint <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kre</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Categories fibred in groupoids (CFGs)</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The purpose of this section is to give an introduction to
the categorical underpinnings of the theory of stacks. We do not
define stacks until Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. However, the
categorical foundation is sufficient to define the cohomology
and Chow rings of stacks,
which we do in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To begin, fix a base scheme <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, the analytically minded might take <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\Spec{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\Spec{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the arithmetically minded could consider <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\Spec{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\Spec{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>, Definition 4.1]</cite>
A <span id="S2.E1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">category fibred in groupoids</span> (CFG) over <math id="S2.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a category
<math id="S2.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> together with a functor <math id="S2.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">i) Given an object <math id="S2.E1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.E1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S2.E1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\rho(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\rho(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S2.E1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.E1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a pullback object <math id="S2.E1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t</annotation></semantics></math> and a morphism
<math id="S2.E1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t\to t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t\to t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.E1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose image under the functor <math id="S2.E1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the morphism <math id="S2.E1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S2.E1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t</annotation></semantics></math> is unique up to canonical isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">ii) If <math id="S2.E1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon t_{1}\to t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon t_{1}\to t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a map in <math id="S2.E1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.E1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\alpha)=T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle id}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\alpha)=T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle id}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.E1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.E1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S2.E1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first condition is just stating that the category
<math id="S2.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> has fibred products relative to <math id="S2.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies i) then <math id="S2.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S2.E2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fibred
category</span>. The second conditions implies that if <math id="S2.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed
scheme then the subcategory <math id="S2.E2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of objects
mapping to <math id="S2.E2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and morphisms mapping to the identity is a <span id="S2.E2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">groupoid</span>; that is all morphisms in <math id="S2.E2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> are
isomorphisms.
Note that if <math id="S2.E2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is an object of <math id="S2.E2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(t)=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(t)=T</annotation></semantics></math>
for some <math id="S2.E2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.E2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.E2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is an object
of
<math id="S2.E2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence every object of <math id="S2.E2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is in
<math id="S2.E2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> for some scheme <math id="S2.E2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second condition may seem a little strange, but it
is a natural one for moduli problems. The concept of
category fibred groupoids over <math id="S2.E2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generalization of the
concept of contravariant functor from <math id="S2.E2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\to\Sets" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\to\Sets</annotation></semantics></math>.
A stack is a CFG that
satisfies certain algebro-geometric conditions analogous to the
conditions satisfied
by a representable functor.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To give a feel for the theory of CFGs we will focus on three examples:
CFGs of smooth and stable curves, representable CFGs, and quotient
CFGs. We will show that the CFGs of smooth and stable curves are
quotient CFGs. This implies that they are algebraic stacks, as
quotient CFGs are always algebraic stacks (although we will not prove
this here).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>CFGs of curves</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the rest of this paper a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">curve</span> will denote a complete (hence projective) scheme of dimension 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Smooth curves)
For any <math id="S2.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category whose objects are <math id="S2.E3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon X\to T</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S2.E3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and <math id="S2.E3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper smooth morphism whose fibers are curves of genus <math id="S2.E3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
A morphism from <math id="S2.E3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.E3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math>
is simply a cartesian diagram.</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;X\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;X\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly <math id="S2.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibred category over <math id="S2.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> by construction. To see that it is fibered in groupoids observe that if</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;X\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
S&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle id}}{{\to}}&amp;S\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>S</mi></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>S</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;X\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
S&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle id}}{{\to}}&amp;S\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.E4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is cartesian then the map <math id="S2.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the fact that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a category fibred in groupoids has
essentially nothing to do with the fact that we are attempting to
parametrize curves of given genus. The only thing we are using is that
morphisms in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are cartesian diagrams.
This shows that, like the notion of
functor, the concept of category fibred in groupoids is very
general.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next we define the CFG of stable curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Stable curves) 
A curve <math id="S2.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of arithmetic genus <math id="S2.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.E5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stable</span> if it is connected, has at worst nodes as singularities and
if every rational component intersects the other components in at
least 3 points.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.E6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.E6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then let <math id="S2.E6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category whose objects
are <math id="S2.E6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon X\to T</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.E6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and <math id="S2.E6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper
flat morphism whose fibers are stable curves of genus <math id="S2.E6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
Morphisms are again cartesian diagrams.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Again <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a CFG containing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as a full subcategory. We can
relax the condition on rational components and obtain CFGs of <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prestable</span> curves. However, if we do so then
we will not obtain a Deligne-Mumford stack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.E7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Pointed curves)
For any <math id="S2.E7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S2.E7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category whose objects
are <math id="S2.E7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon X\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of smooth curves and
<math id="S2.E7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint sections of <math id="S2.E7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
A morphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi^{\prime}}}{{\to}}T^{\prime},\sigma_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,\sigma_{n}^{\prime})\to(X^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mover><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi^{\prime}}}{{\to}}T^{\prime},\sigma_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,\sigma_{n}^{\prime})\to(X^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.E7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a cartesian
diagram</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;X\\
\pi^{\prime}\downarrow&amp;&amp;\pi\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle g}}{{\to}}&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>g</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;X\\
\pi^{\prime}\downarrow&amp;&amp;\pi\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle g}}{{\to}}&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.E7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S2.E7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ\sigma_{i}^{\prime}=\sigma_{i}\circ g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ\sigma_{i}^{\prime}=\sigma_{i}\circ g</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.E7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.E7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> then we define <math id="S2.E7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the CFG whose
objects are <math id="S2.E7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.E7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and flat and the fibers of <math id="S2.E7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> are connected
curves with at worst nodes as signularities and the <math id="S2.E7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are
again disjoint sections of <math id="S2.E7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. We impose the additional condition
that every rational component of a fiber must have a total of at least
3 points marked by the sections plus intersections with the other
components. A morphism is once again cartesian diagram compatible
with the sections.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The CFG <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a full subcategory of the CFG <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> when
<math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We may also relax the stability condition to obtain
the CFG of <span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prestable pointed curves</span> which also contains <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> as a
full subcategory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.E8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All CFGs of curves are subcategories of the the “universal” CFG
<math id="S2.E8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mor({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mor</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mor({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Objects of <math id="S2.E8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mor({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mor</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mor({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> are morphisms of <math id="S2.E8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes and
morphisms in <math id="S2.E8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mor({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mor</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mor({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> are cartesian diagrams of <math id="S2.E8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes. The
CFGs
<math id="S2.E8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are full subcategories of <math id="S2.E8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mor({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mor</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mor({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> but
<math id="S2.E8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.E8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are not.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Representable CFGs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\colon{\mathcal{S}}\to\Sets" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\colon{\mathcal{S}}\to\Sets</annotation></semantics></math> be a contravariant functor. There is an
associated category fibred in groupoids <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a
scheme <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> an object of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{F}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{F}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is
simply an element of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in F(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in F(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> there is a
morphism <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\to t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\to t</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> under the
map of sets <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(T^{\prime})\to F(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(T^{\prime})\to F(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. With this construction if <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme then the groupoid <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{F}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{F}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is the category whose
objects are the elements of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and all morphisms are identities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular if <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme then we can associate to its
functor of points a CFG <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is simply the category
of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes viewed as a fibred category over the category of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.E9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A CFG <math id="S2.E9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.E9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">representable</span> if <math id="S2.E9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
equivalent to a CFG <math id="S2.E9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.E9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.E9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.E10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Yoneda’s lemma implies that if <math id="S2.E10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are
schemes
then there is an isomorphism of schemes <math id="S2.E10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there
is an equivalence of categories <math id="S2.E10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{X}\to\underline{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{X}\to\underline{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.E11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The existence of curves of every genus with non-trivial automorphism
implies that the CFG <math id="S2.E11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is not representable (i.e., not the CFG
associated to a scheme) because if <math id="S2.E11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a field then the category
<math id="S2.E11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}(\Spec k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}(\Spec k)</annotation></semantics></math> is not equivalent to one where all morphisms are
identities.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.E12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.E12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.E12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a CFG and <math id="S2.E12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.E12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme then to give an object
of <math id="S2.E12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to giving a functor
<math id="S2.E12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with the projection
functor to <math id="S2.E12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given an object <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> define a
functor <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> by mapping a
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to a pullback of the object <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> via the morphism <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Note that the definition of this functor requires a
choice for each pullback. However, different choices give rise to
equivalent functors). Conversely, given a functor <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> we set <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to be the image of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.E13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.E13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme and <math id="S2.E13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a CFG we will streamline the
notation by writing <math id="S2.E13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> in lieu of <math id="S2.E13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{T}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.E14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Proposition <a href="#S2.E12" title="Proposition 2.12. ‣ 2.2. Representable CFGs ‣ 2. Categories fibred in groupoids (CFGs) ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a> giving a family of smooth curves <math id="S2.E14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.E14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to giving a map <math id="S2.E14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this way <math id="S2.E14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the “classifying
space” for smooth curves. Similarly <math id="S2.E14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> classifies
stable curves.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Curves and quotient CFGs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear algebraic group; i.e., a closed subgroup of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
for some <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity we assume that <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth over <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\Spec{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\Spec{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> this is automatic, but the
assumption is necessary in positive or mixed characteristic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.E15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.E15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a scheme. A <math id="S2.E15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor over <math id="S2.E15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth morphism
<math id="S2.E15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.E15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on <math id="S2.E15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.E15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.E15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant and there is an
isomorphism of <math id="S2.E15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-spaces <math id="S2.E15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{T}E\to G\times E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{T}E\to G\times E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.E16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.E16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG</annotation></semantics></math> be the CFG whose objects are <math id="S2.E16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsors
<math id="S2.E16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and whose morphism are cartesian diagrams</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.E16.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with the added condition that the map <math id="S2.E16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\to E</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.E16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, if <math id="S2.E16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme and <math id="S2.E16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic group acting on <math id="S2.E16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
then we define a CFG <math id="S2.E16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> to be the category whose objects are
pairs <math id="S2.E16.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(E\to T,E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E\to T,E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}X)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E16.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.E16.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor and <math id="S2.E16.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon E\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.E16.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map.
A morphism <math id="S2.E16.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(E^{\prime}\to T^{\prime},E^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f^{\prime}}}{{\to}}X)\to(E\to T,E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mover><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E^{\prime}\to T^{\prime},E^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f^{\prime}}}{{\to}}X)\to(E\to T,E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.E16.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a cartesian diagram of torsors</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle h}}{{\to}}&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle h}}{{\to}}&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.E16.p2.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S2.E16.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}=f\circ h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}=f\circ h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.17</span></span><span id="S2.E17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A CFG is <math id="S2.E17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.E17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quotient CFG</span> if <math id="S2.E17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
equivalent to a CFG <math id="S2.E17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> for some scheme <math id="S2.E17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.18</span></span><span id="S2.E18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.E18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme then <math id="S2.E18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent
to the quotient CFG <math id="S2.E18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(G\times X)/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(G\times X)/G]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S2.E18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times X</annotation></semantics></math>
by the rule <math id="S2.E18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(g^{\prime},x)=(gg^{\prime},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(g^{\prime},x)=(gg^{\prime},x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.19</span></span><span id="S2.E19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although we have not yet discussed the geometry of CFGs the geometry of a quotient CFG <math id="S2.E19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.E19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant geometry of <math id="S2.E19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. This point of view will be emphasized in our discussion of cohomology rings.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1. </span><math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the definition of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as a CFG is purely categorical, the
fact that if <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is a family
of smooth curves then <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projective</span> morphism allows us to prove that <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient
CFG.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To state the result we introduce some notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.20</span></span><span id="S2.E20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an integer <math id="S2.E20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S2.E20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hilbert scheme of one dimensional subschemes of
<math id="S2.E20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}</annotation></semantics></math> with Hilbert polynomial <math id="S2.E20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(6t-1)(g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(6t-1)(g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The action of
<math id="S2.E20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S2.E20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a corresponding action on the Hilbert scheme.
If <math id="S2.E20.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then the canonical divisor on any curve <math id="S2.E20.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is ample and <math id="S2.E20.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3K_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3K_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is very ample. Let
<math id="S2.E20.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the locally closed subscheme of <math id="S2.E20.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to smooth curves
<math id="S2.E20.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.E20.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(1)\simeq\omega_{C}^{\otimes 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(1)\simeq\omega_{C}^{\otimes 3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.21</span></span><span id="S2.E21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.E21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an equivalence of categories <math id="S2.E21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.E21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We define a functor <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a
family <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth curves consider the
rank <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5g-6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5g-6)</annotation></semantics></math> projective space bundle
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}(\pi_{*}(\omega_{X/T}^{\otimes 3}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}(\pi_{*}(\omega_{X/T}^{\otimes 3}))</annotation></semantics></math> whose
fiber at a point <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in T</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete linear series <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|3K_{X_{\pi^{-1}(t)}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|3K_{X_{\pi^{-1}(t)}}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> be
the associated <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> torsor. The pullback of
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}(\pi_{*}(\omega_{X/T}^{\otimes 3}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}(\pi_{*}(\omega_{X/T}^{\otimes 3}))</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial and
defines an embedding of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{T}E\hookrightarrow{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>E</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{T}E\hookrightarrow{\mathbb{P}}^{5g-6}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we obtain a morphism <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
construction is natural so the map <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with the
natural <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> action on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given
a morphism</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;X\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}X^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;X\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">our construction gives a morphism of
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math>-torsors</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
T^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">compatible with the maps <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\to H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\to H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now check that our functor is an equivalence by defining a functor
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
as follows:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a torsor <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and a map <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> we
obtain a family of projective curves <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to E</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an action of
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> such that the morphism <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to E</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant. By definition of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> as the total space of a
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor the action <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is free. Since there is a
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant morphism <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to E</annotation></semantics></math> it follows that the action of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is also free. We would like to let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the quotient <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> by
the free <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> action. Unfortunately, there is no </span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a
priori</span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text"> reason why the quotient of a scheme (even a projective or
quasi-projective scheme)
by the free action of an algebraic group exists in the category of
schemes<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  One can prove, using a non-trivial theorem of Deligne
and Mumford, that such a quotient automatically exists as an
algebraic space.</span></span></span>. However, we are in a relatively special situation
in that we already know that a quotient <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E/\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E/\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> exists as a
scheme (since it’s equal to <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>) and the morphism <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to E</annotation></semantics></math> is a </span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projective</span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text"> morphism. Descent theory for projective morphisms
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MFK</a>, Proposition 7.1]</cite>) implies that there is a quotient <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=Z/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=Z/G</annotation></semantics></math> which is projective over <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=Z/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=Z/G</annotation></semantics></math> and such that the diagram</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}Z&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
X&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>Z</mi></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}Z&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
X&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">is cartesian.
Hence <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of smooth curves over <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
A similar analysis defines the image of a morphism in
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and one can check that there are natural transformations <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\circ p\to Id_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\circ p\to Id_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\circ q\to Id_{[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\circ q\to Id_{[H_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2. </span><math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key fact about stable curves, and the reason that the theory of
stable curve is so elegant, is the following result of Deligne and Mumford.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.22</span></span><span id="S2.E22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.E22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a><span id="S2.E22.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Thm 2.1]</span></cite><span id="S2.E22.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If <math id="S2.E22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of stable curves then the dualizing sheaf <math id="S2.E22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is
locally free and relatively ample. Moreover, the sheaf <math id="S2.E22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\pi}^{\otimes 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\pi}^{\otimes 3}</annotation></semantics></math> is relatively very
ample; i.e., if <math id="S2.E22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a point of <math id="S2.E22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}=\pi^{-1}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}=\pi^{-1}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> then the line
bundle
<math id="S2.E22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{X_{p}}^{\otimes 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{X_{p}}^{\otimes 3}</annotation></semantics></math> is very ample.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The same argument used in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.E21" title="Proposition 2.21. ‣ 2.3.1. 
            
              
                
                M
                g
              
            
           is a quotient CFG ‣ 2.3. Curves and quotient CFGs ‣ 2. Categories fibred in groupoids (CFGs) ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a>
can now be used to prove that the CFG <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.23</span></span><span id="S2.E23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.E23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.E23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the locally closed subscheme of <math id="S2.E23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing
embedded stable curves <math id="S2.E23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{C}(1)\simeq\omega_{C}^{\otimes 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{C}(1)\simeq\omega_{C}^{\otimes 3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is an
equivalence of categories <math id="S2.E23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}\to[\overline{H}_{g}/\PGL(5g-5)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>PGL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}\to[\overline{H}_{g}/\PGL(5g-5)]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.3. </span>Curves of very low genus</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section we only considered curves of genus <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The purpose of this section is to briefly discuss the CFGs of
curves of genus 0 and 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.24</span></span><span id="S2.E24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Curves of genus 0)<span id="S2.E24.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.E24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the CFG of nodal curves of
arithmetic genus 0. A nodal curve of genus 0 is necessarily a tree of
<math id="S2.E24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>s. However, there is no bound on the number of components. The
CFG <math id="S2.E24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be stratified by the number of nodes (or equivalently
irreducible components). Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful2</a>]</cite> we denote by <math id="S2.E24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}</annotation></semantics></math> the CFG whose objects are families of rational nodal curves with
at most <math id="S2.E24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> nodes. The CFG <math id="S2.E24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth rational curves is
equivalent to <math id="S2.E24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\PGL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\PGL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">EF1</a>, Proposition 6]</cite> it was shown that
<math id="S2.E24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the CFG <math id="S2.E24.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/\GL_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>GL</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/\GL_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E24.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the
set of quadratic forms in 3 variables with rank at least 2. On the
other hand there are families of rational curves <math id="S2.E24.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E24.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is not a projective morphism. In fact, Fulghesu
constructed examples where <math id="S2.E24.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme and <math id="S2.E24.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is only an
algebraic space. Despite the
pathological behavior, Fulghesu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful2</a>]</cite>
proved that <math id="S2.E24.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic
stack for any <math id="S2.E24.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recently Kresch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kre</a>, Proposition 5.2]</cite> proved that for <math id="S2.E24.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.E24.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}</annotation></semantics></math> is not equivalent to a quotient of the form <math id="S2.E24.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Z/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z/G]</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S2.E24.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> an algebraic space; ie <math id="S2.E24.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq k}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a quotient stack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E24.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, if <math id="S2.E24.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> then the CFGs <math id="S2.E24.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.E24.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are well understood. They are represented by
non-singular projective schemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knu</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.25</span></span><span id="S2.E25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Curves of genus 1)<span id="S2.E25.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The CFG <math id="S2.E25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, of curves of genus 1, is rather strange. Its behavior
highlights the distinction between curves of genus 1 and elliptic
curves. An elliptic curve is a curve of genus 1 together with a point
chosen to be the origin for the group law. In particular an elliptic
curve is a projective algebraic group, while a curve of genus 1 is a
torsor for this group.
Given a curve <math id="S2.E25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus 1 together with a choice of a point <math id="S2.E25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\in C</annotation></semantics></math> the group scheme <math id="S2.E25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,O)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,O)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S2.E25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the
automorphism group of a curve is not a linear algebraic group. Thus
the CFG <math id="S2.E25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be equivalent to a CFG of the form
<math id="S2.E25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.E25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a linear algebraic group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E25.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E25.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the CFG <math id="S2.E25.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> of elliptic curves behaves a lot like
the CFGs <math id="S2.E25.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.E25.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, if <math id="S2.E25.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of smooth curves of genus 1 with section
<math id="S2.E25.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> the direct image <math id="S2.E25.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}(O_{X}(\sigma)^{\otimes 3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}(O_{X}(\sigma)^{\otimes 3})</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally free
sheaf of rank 3. An argument similar to the Proof of Proposition
<a href="#S2.E21" title="Proposition 2.21. ‣ 2.3.1. 
            
              
                
                M
                g
              
            
           is a quotient CFG ‣ 2.3. Curves and quotient CFGs ‣ 2. Categories fibred in groupoids (CFGs) ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a>
can be used to show that <math id="S2.E25.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}=[U/\PGL_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}=[U/\PGL_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E25.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the open set of smooth plane cubics. Similarly <math id="S2.E25.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}=[W/\PGL_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}=[W/\PGL_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.E25.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is the open set of plane cubics with at worst nodes as
singularities.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Fiber products of CFGs and universal curves</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far we have not discussed how universal curves fit into the CFG
picture. In order to do so we need to introduce another categorical
notion - the fiber product of two CFGs over a third CFG. Once this is
done, we can explain why <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal
curve over <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying this fact inductively we can show that the
CFGs
<math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are all quotient CFGs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.1. </span>Fiber products of CFGs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since CFGs are categories the natural home for the class of all CFGs
over <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-category</span>. A 2-category has objects
(in our case the CFGs), morphisms (functors between the CFGs) and
morphisms between morphisms (natural transformations of functors).
For the most part this added level of complexity can be ignored, but
it does show up in one of the most important categorical
constructions, the fiber product of two CFGs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.26</span></span><span id="S2.E26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.E26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> be CFGs over our fixed category <math id="S2.E26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the
fiber product <math id="S2.E26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be
the CFG whose objects
are triples <math id="S2.E26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is an object of <math id="S2.E26.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.E26.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>
is an object of <math id="S2.E26.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E26.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism in
<math id="S2.E26.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
between <math id="S2.E26.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E26.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E26.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E26.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism in <math id="S2.E26.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> between
<math id="S2.E26.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\prime},y^{\prime},\phi^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\prime},y^{\prime},\phi^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E26.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a pair of morphisms
<math id="S2.E26.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon x^{\prime}\to x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon x^{\prime}\to x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.E26.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\colon y^{\prime}\to y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\colon y^{\prime}\to y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.E26.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ f(\alpha)=g(\beta)\circ\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ f(\alpha)=g(\beta)\circ\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.27</span></span><span id="S2.E27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E27.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A straightforward check shows that <math id="S2.E27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed a CFG. There are also obvious functors <math id="S2.E27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}\colon{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}\colon{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y}\colon{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y}\colon{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> but the compositions <math id="S2.E27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E27.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ p_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ p_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are not equal as functors <math id="S2.E27.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S2.E27.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ p_{X}(x,y,\theta)=f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ p_{X}(x,y,\theta)=f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E27.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ p_{Y}(x,y,\theta)=g(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ p_{Y}(x,y,\theta)=g(y)</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic but not
not necessarily the same objects of <math id="S2.E27.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, there
is a natural transformation of functors <math id="S2.E27.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\colon f\circ p_{X}\to g\circ p_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\colon f\circ p_{X}\to g\circ p_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this reason we say that the
diagram</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{Y}}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{Y}}\\
p_{X}\downarrow&amp;&amp;g\downarrow\\
{\mathcal{X}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{Z}}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mover></mtd><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}{\mathcal{X}}\times_{\mathcal{Z}}{\mathcal{Y}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{Y}}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{Y}}\\
p_{X}\downarrow&amp;&amp;g\downarrow\\
{\mathcal{X}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{Z}}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.E27.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is <span id="S2.E27.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-cartesian</span>. In general a diagram of CFGs</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}{\mathcal{W}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle g}}{{\to}}&amp;\mathcal{X}\\
p\downarrow&amp;&amp;q\downarrow\\
{\mathcal{Y}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{Z}}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>g</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}{\mathcal{W}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle g}}{{\to}}&amp;\mathcal{X}\\
p\downarrow&amp;&amp;q\downarrow\\
{\mathcal{Y}}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{Z}}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.E27.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is <span id="S2.E27.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-commutative</span> if there is a natural transformation between the
functors <math id="S2.E27.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\circ g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\circ g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E27.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">So far our discussion of CFGs has been very categorical. The next
definition is the first step towards connecting the theory of CFGs
with algebraic geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.28</span></span><span id="S2.E28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E28.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism of CFGs <math id="S2.E28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.E28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">representable</span>
if for every scheme <math id="S2.E28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and morphism <math id="S2.E28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber
product of CFGs
<math id="S2.E28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a scheme.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.29</span></span><span id="S2.E29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E29.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This definition is saying that although <math id="S2.E29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.E29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> are not represented by schemes the fibers of the morphism are
schemes. It also allows us to define algebro-geometric properties of
representable morphisms of CFGs. If <span id="S2.E29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span> is a property of
morphisms of schemes which is
preserved by base change<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                  
                  
                  
                This covers many of the most common
types of morphisms encountered in algebraic geometry. For example
the properties of being separated, finite, proper, flat and smooth can
are all preserved by base change.</span></span></span>
then a representable morphism <math id="S2.E29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> has
property <span id="S2.E29.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span> if for every map of scheme <math id="S2.E29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> the map of schemes
<math id="S2.E29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> has property <span id="S2.E29.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.30</span></span><span id="S2.E30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E30.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.E30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic group acting on a scheme <math id="S2.E30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> then there is a morphism <math id="S2.E30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>
defined as follows. Given a map of schemes <math id="S2.E30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon T\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon T\to X</annotation></semantics></math> (an object of the
category <span id="S2.E30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">X</span>) we can consider the trivial torsor <math id="S2.E30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times T\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times T\to T</annotation></semantics></math> together with the <math id="S2.E30.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map <math id="S2.E30.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,t)\mapsto gf(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,t)\mapsto gf(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to define an object of <math id="S2.E30.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>. From this definition it is clear
that a morphism of <math id="S2.E30.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S2.E30.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a morphism in
the CFG <math id="S2.E30.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E30.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E30.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that the map <math id="S2.E30.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is representable. In fact if <math id="S2.E30.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism corresponding to a <math id="S2.E30.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor <math id="S2.E30.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> with
equivariant map <math id="S2.E30.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon E\to X</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{[X/G]}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{[X/G]}X</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by the scheme <math id="S2.E30.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us see this
explicitly.
An object of <math id="S2.E30.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T\times_{[X/G]}X)(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T\times_{[X/G]}X)(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is a triple
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},f_{2},\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},f_{2},\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.E30.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{1}}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{1}}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S2.E30.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S2.E30.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, <math id="S2.E30.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{2}}}{{\to}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{2}}}{{\to}}X</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S2.E30.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S2.E30.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism in <math id="S2.E30.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> between the two images of <math id="S2.E30.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The image of <math id="S2.E30.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{1}}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{1}}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is the torsor <math id="S2.E30.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.E30.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map the composition <math id="S2.E30.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to E\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. The image of <math id="S2.E30.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{2}}}{{\to}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{2}}}{{\to}}X</annotation></semantics></math> is the trivial
torsor
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with equivariant map <math id="S2.E30.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,t^{\prime})\mapsto gf_{2}(t^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,t^{\prime})\mapsto gf_{2}(t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The isomorphism <math id="S2.E30.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.E30.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> gives an isomorphism between the
trivial torsor
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the pullback torsor <math id="S2.E30.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words <math id="S2.E30.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> determines a trivialization of the pullback torsor
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The trivialization gives a section <math id="S2.E30.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to E\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to E\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Composing the section with the projection <math id="S2.E30.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to E</annotation></semantics></math> gives a map <math id="S2.E30.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to E</annotation></semantics></math>; i.e., an object of <math id="S2.E30.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. A
similar, if more involved, analysis shows that morphisms in the category <math id="S2.E30.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{[X/G]}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{[X/G]}X</annotation></semantics></math> determine morphisms of <math id="S2.E30.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes. In this way
we obtain a functor
<math id="S2.E30.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{[X/G]}X\to\underline{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{[X/G]}X\to\underline{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. This functor is in equivalence
of categories. To see this note that if <math id="S2.E30.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.E30.p2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme then the map <math id="S2.E30.p2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>
determines a trivialization of the pullback torsor <math id="S2.E30.p2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G\times X)_{X}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G\times X)_{X}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
and thus an object of <math id="S2.E30.p2.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T\times_{[X/G]}X)(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T\times_{[X/G]}X)(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.31</span></span><span id="S2.E31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E31.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fully faithful inclusion functor <math id="S2.E31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is
represented
by open immersions. Thus we say that <math id="S2.E31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open subCFG of
<math id="S2.E31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.2. </span>Smooth pointed curves and the universal curve</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a an obvious morphism of CFGs <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that forgets
the section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.32</span></span><span id="S2.E32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E32.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.E32.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.E32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism corresponding to a family of
smooth curves of genus <math id="S2.E32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.E32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math> then the fiber product <math id="S2.E32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by the scheme <math id="S2.E32.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the
map <math id="S2.E32.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable and smooth and we can view <math id="S2.E32.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>
as the universal curve over <math id="S2.E32.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch of proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">An object of the fiber product <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the following data: a
morphism <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>, a family of pointed curves <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with
section <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and isomorphism in <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of the pullback family <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the family <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has a section,
the projection <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> also has
a section. Composing this section with the projection <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{T}T^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> gives a map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., an object <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{X}(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{X}(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Again, a straightforward (if tedious) check shows that this procedure maps
morphisms in the category <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> to morphisms in the category
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. This defines a functor
which is an equivalence of categories.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.3. </span>Stable pointed curves and the universal curve</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A similar argument can be used to show that <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
universal stable curve over <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this argument requires
more care because the morphism of CFGs <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
is not as tautological as the corresponding morphism for smooth
curves. The reason is that if <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(X\to T,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X\to T,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of
pointed stable curves, the corresponding family of curves <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math>
need not be stable as it may have rational components which intersect
the other components in only 2 points. To define a functor
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> we must show that given such family of
curves <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math>, we may contract the offending rational components in
the fibers of <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. This analysis was carried out by F. Knudsen
in his paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knu</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.33</span></span><span id="S2.E33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Knudsen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knu</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.E33.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.E33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.E33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
there is a representable contraction morphism <math id="S2.E33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> that makes <math id="S2.E33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> into the universal curve over <math id="S2.E33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.E34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.34</span></span><span id="S2.E34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E34.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is relatively easy to show that
<math id="S2.E34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0,3}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,3}=\Spec S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0,3}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,3}=\Spec S</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from Knudsen’s results
that for <math id="S2.E34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> the CFGs <math id="S2.E34.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are all represented by schemes.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Theorem <a href="#S2.E33" title="Theorem 2.33 (Knudsen []). ‣ 2.4.3. Stable pointed curves and the universal curve ‣ 2.4. Fiber products of CFGs and universal curves ‣ 2. Categories fibred in groupoids (CFGs) ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.33</span></a> with the following proposition
shows that CFGs of pointed stable curves of genus <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> are quotient CFGs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.E35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.E35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.35</span></span><span id="S2.E35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.E35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.E35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.E35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.E35.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG and <math id="S2.E35.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a representable morphism
then <math id="S2.E35.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the CFG <math id="S2.E35.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Y/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Y/G]</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.E35.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(Sketch) Since <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber product
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{{\mathcal{X}}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{{\mathcal{X}}}X</annotation></semantics></math> is represented <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times X</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this
identification the two projection maps <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{{\mathcal{X}}}X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{{\mathcal{X}}}X\to X</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the projection <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon G\times X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon G\times X\to X</annotation></semantics></math> and
the action morphism <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon G\times X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon G\times X\to X</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,x)\mapsto gx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,x)\mapsto gx</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
represents <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> then the fiber
product <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\times_{\mathcal{Y}}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\times_{\mathcal{Y}}Y</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under this identification, one of the projections <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\times_{{\mathcal{Y}}}Y\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\times_{{\mathcal{Y}}}Y\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the usual projection <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times Y\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times Y\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> and the other gives an action map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times Y\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times Y\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that
the map of schemes <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a morphism <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> the composition with the
morphism of CFGs <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we obtain a <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> together
with an equivariant map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. The total space <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> represents
the fiber product <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{\mathcal{X}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{\mathcal{X}}X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the morphism <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through the morphism <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the fiber product <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{\mathcal{X}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{\mathcal{X}}X</annotation></semantics></math> is
equivalent to the fiber product <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{\mathcal{Y}}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{\mathcal{Y}}Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we
obtain a morphism <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> which can be checked to be
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant. Hence an object of <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> produces an
object of <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Y/G](T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Y/G](T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Further analysis shows that we can extend
this construction to define a functor <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}\to[Y/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}\to[Y/G]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The construction of a functor <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Y/G]\to{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Y/G]\to{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is more subtle.
Given a <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map to <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> the composition <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we obtain a morphism
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>. We would like to lift this to a morphism
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable the fiber product <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is represented by a scheme <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction there are projections
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so to define a morphism <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> it suffices to construct a section of the projection map
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}=E\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}=E\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have a cartesian diagram
where the horizontal maps are <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsors</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T^{\prime}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
E&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}E^{\prime}&amp;\to&amp;T^{\prime}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
E&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=T\times_{\mathcal{X}}{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber product
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}=E\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}=E\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified
with the fiber product of schemes <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{X}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{X}Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to X</annotation></semantics></math> factors through the <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant
map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> so the projection <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\to E</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant section <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence there is an induced map of quotients <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a section to the map <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>.
The composition <math id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to T^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to T^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is our desired morphism.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now come to heart of this article - the definition of the integral
cohomology ring of a CFG. Although this definition is very formal,
the cohomology ring of the CFG <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> naturally contains all
tautological classes. Moreover, if <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG
we will show that <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=H^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=H^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
is the <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant cohomology ring of <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this section we assume that the ground scheme <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the
spectrum of a field. When we work with cohomology we assume <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\Spec{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\Spec{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the dimension of a variety is its complex dimension.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Motivation and definition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since a CFG is not a space, we need an indirect method to define
cohomology classes on a CFG. To do this we start with a simple
observation. Since cohomology is a contravariant functor, a cohomology
class <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in H^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> determines a pullback cohomology class <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)\in H^{*}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)\in H^{*}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> for every map <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X</annotation></semantics></math>. (Here we can simply let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
be topological spaces.)
Moreover, functoriality also ensures that the classes <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy an
obvious compatibility condition. Given morphisms <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover><mi>T</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(ft)=f^{*}c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(ft)=f^{*}c(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
With this motivation we make the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a CFG defined over <math id="S3.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A cohomology class <math id="S3.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the data of a cohomology class <math id="S3.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)\in H^{*}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)\in H^{*}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> for every scheme
<math id="S3.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and every
object <math id="S3.E1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.E1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. The classes <math id="S3.E1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)</annotation></semantics></math> should satisfy the following
compatibility condition:
Given schemes <math id="S3.E1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and objects <math id="S3.E1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.E1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and a morphism <math id="S3.E1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\to t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\to t</annotation></semantics></math> whose image in <math id="S3.E1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a morphism <math id="S3.E1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S3.E1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}c(t)=c(t^{\prime})\in H^{*}(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}c(t)=c(t^{\prime})\in H^{*}(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The cup product on cohomology of spaces guarantees that the collection
of all cohomology classes on <math id="S3.E1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a graded
skew-commutative ring. We denote
this ring by <math id="S3.E1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1. </span>Chow cohomology of CFGs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since many naturally occurring classes in the cohomology of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
are algebraic we also define the Chow cohomology ring of a CFG
defined over an arbitrary field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful3</a>, Definition 17.3]</cite>
Let <math id="S3.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a scheme. A Chow cohomology class <math id="S3.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S3.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
is an assignment, for every map <math id="S3.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X</annotation></semantics></math>, a map on Chow groups
<math id="S3.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)\colon A_{*}(T)\to A_{*-i}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)\colon A_{*}(T)\to A_{*-i}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <math id="S3.E2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are
compatible with the usual operations in intersection theory (flat
pullback and lci pullback, proper pushforward, etc.). The group of Chow cohomology classes of codimension <math id="S3.E2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted <math id="S3.E2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.E2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in A_{k}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in A_{k}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.E2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in A^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in A^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> then we use the notation <math id="S3.E2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\cap\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\cap\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to denote
the image of <math id="S3.E2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> under the map <math id="S3.E2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)\colon A_{k}(T)\to A_{k-i}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)\colon A_{k}(T)\to A_{k-i}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Composition of maps makes the collection of operations of all
codimension into a graded ring which
we denote <math id="S3.E2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X):=\oplus A^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X):=\oplus A^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> admits a resolution of
singularities then <math id="S3.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful3</a>, Example
17.4.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The relationship between Chow cohomology and the usual Chow groups is
given by the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Poincaré Duality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful3</a>, Corollary 17.4]</cite>)
If <math id="S3.E4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth variety of dimension <math id="S3.E4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
then the map
<math id="S3.E4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}(X)\to A_{n-i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}(X)\to A_{n-i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.E4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\mapsto c(id)\cap[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\mapsto c(id)\cap[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism. Moreover, the ring structure on <math id="S3.E4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> given by
composition of operations is compatible with the ring structure on
<math id="S3.E4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> given by intersection product.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a map <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> there is a natural pullback
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon A^{*}(X)\to A^{*}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon A^{*}(X)\to A^{*}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}c(T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}Y)=c(ft)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}c(T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}Y)=c(ft)</annotation></semantics></math>. This functoriality allows us to define the Chow cohomology
ring of a CFG:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a CFG, then a Chow cohomology class
is an assignment for every scheme <math id="S3.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and every object <math id="S3.E5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S3.E5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> a Chow cohomology
<math id="S3.E5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)\in A^{*}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)\in A^{*}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the same compatibility conditions as
in Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Motivation and definition ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.E6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.E6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a map of CFGs over
<math id="S3.E6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> then
there is a pullback homomorphism <math id="S3.E6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}\colon H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})\to H^{*}({\mathcal{Y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}\colon H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})\to H^{*}({\mathcal{Y}})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.E6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is an object
of <math id="S3.E6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S3.E6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}c(t)=c(p\circ t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}c(t)=c(p\circ t))</annotation></semantics></math>. In fancier language, <math id="S3.E6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a functor from
the 2-category of CFGs to the category of skew-commutative
rings. Similar functoriality holds for the Chow cohomology groups.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To connect our theory with the usual cohomology theory schemes over
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.E7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.E7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=\underline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=\underline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.E7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a scheme then
<math id="S3.E7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=H^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Likewise <math id="S3.E7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a class <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in H^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> we define a corresponding class in
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)=t^{*}c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)=t^{*}c</annotation></semantics></math> for every morphism <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}X</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, given a class <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>
then <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(id)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(id)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a class in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.E8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.E8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG and <math id="S3.E8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.E8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant
vector bundle on <math id="S3.E8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.E8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> defines Chern classes <math id="S3.E8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}(V)\in H^{2i}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}(V)\in H^{2i}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S3.E8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>) as follows:
Suppose <math id="S3.E8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\stackrel{{\scriptstyle t}}{{\to}}{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a torsor
<math id="S3.E8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> and equivariant map <math id="S3.E8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.E8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is
a <math id="S3.E8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor the pullback <math id="S3.E8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an equivalence of categories
between vector bundles on <math id="S3.E8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.E8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant vector bundles on <math id="S3.E8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define <math id="S3.E8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}(V)(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}(V)(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the <math id="S3.E8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th Chern class of
the vector bundle on <math id="S3.E8.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the <math id="S3.E8.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant vector bundle
<math id="S3.E8.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}V</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.E8.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2. </span>Tautological classes and boundary classes</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most familiar classes on the moduli space of curves are naturally defined
as elements of the cohomology or Chow cohomology ring of the CFGs
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.
Precisely we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.E9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<math id="S3.E9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> classes)
The assignment which assigns to any family of stable curves <math id="S3.E9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon X\to T</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to an object <math id="S3.E9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>
the class <math id="S3.E9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}(t)=c_{i}(\pi_{*}(\omega_{X/T}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}(t)=c_{i}(\pi_{*}(\omega_{X/T}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}(t)=\pi_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{X/T})^{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}(t)=\pi_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{X/T})^{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> define classes <math id="S3.E9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.E9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S3.E9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Functoriality of <math id="S3.E9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that these classes pullback to classes
in
<math id="S3.E9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-classes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.E10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<math id="S3.E10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> classes)
The assignment to any family of stable curves <math id="S3.E10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon X\to T</annotation></semantics></math>
with sections <math id="S3.E10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to an object
<math id="S3.E10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>
the classes <math id="S3.E10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}(t)=c_{1}(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{X/T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}(t)=c_{1}(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{X/T})</annotation></semantics></math> define
classes <math id="S3.E10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.E11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(boundary classes)
The deformation theory of curves implies that
the locus in <math id="S3.E11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing tri-canonically embedded curves
with nodes is a normal crossing divisor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>, Corollary 1.9]</cite>.
Since <math id="S3.E11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is
non-singular this is a Cartier divisor. Let <math id="S3.E11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{0}(\overline{H}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{0}(\overline{H}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
be the divisor parametrizing irreducible nodal curves, and for <math id="S3.E11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq[g/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq[g/2]</annotation></semantics></math> let
<math id="S3.E11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}(\overline{H}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}(\overline{H}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
the divisor parametrizing curves that are the union of a component of
genus <math id="S3.E11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and one of genus <math id="S3.E11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-i</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of these divisors is
<math id="S3.E11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant and thus defines a <math id="S3.E11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant line bundle
<math id="S3.E11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\Delta_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\Delta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S3.E11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the construction of Example <a href="#S3.E8" title="Example 3.8. ‣ 3.1.1. Chow cohomology of CFGs ‣ 3.1. Motivation and definition ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> yields
boundary classes <math id="S3.E11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}=c_{1}(L(\Delta_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}=c_{1}(L(\Delta_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E11.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S3.E11.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.E12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.E12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> classes can be also be defined via a vector
bundle on <math id="S3.E12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as was the case for the <math id="S3.E12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S3.E12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{H}\colon\overline{Z}_{g}\to\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{H}\colon\overline{Z}_{g}\to\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal family of tri-canonically
embedded stable curves. Then <math id="S3.E12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{H})_{*}(\omega_{\overline{Z}_{g}/\overline{H}_{g}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{H})_{*}(\omega_{\overline{Z}_{g}/\overline{H}_{g}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S3.E12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant vector bundle <math id="S3.E12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the <math id="S3.E12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th Chern class of this bundle as defined in Example
<a href="#S3.E8" title="Example 3.8. ‣ 3.1.1. Chow cohomology of CFGs ‣ 3.1. Motivation and definition ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will see in the next section that every class in <math id="S3.E12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
(resp. <math id="S3.E12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>) may be defined in terms of equivariant
cohomology (resp Chow) classes on <math id="S3.E12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Quotient CFGs and equivariant cohomology</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of this section is to show the cohomology ring
of a CFG <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the equivariant cohomology
(resp. Chow) ring of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We begin by recalling the definition of
equivariant cohomology.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span>Equivariant cohomology</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equivariant cohomology was classically defined using the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Borel
construction</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.E13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.E13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a topological group and
let <math id="S3.E13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> be a contractible space on which <math id="S3.E13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely. If <math id="S3.E13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S3.E13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-space then we define the equivariant cohomology ring <math id="S3.E13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to be
the cohomology of the quotient space <math id="S3.E13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}EG</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.E13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts on
<math id="S3.E13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times EG</annotation></semantics></math> by the rule <math id="S3.E13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x,v)=(gx,gv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x,v)=(gx,gv)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.E14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The definition is independent of the choice of the contractible space
<math id="S3.E14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.E14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on <math id="S3.E14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with quotient <math id="S3.E14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/G</annotation></semantics></math> then there is an
isomorphism
<math id="S3.E14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}EG\to X/G\times EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}EG\to X/G\times EG</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.E14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible
it follows that <math id="S3.E14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{G}(X)=H^{*}(X/G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{G}(X)=H^{*}(X/G)</annotation></semantics></math>. At the other extreme, if
<math id="S3.E14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S3.E14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.E14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}EG=X\times BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}EG=X\times BG</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.E14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG=EG/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG=EG/G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{G}(X)=H^{*}(X)\otimes H^{*}(BG)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{G}(X)=H^{*}(X)\otimes H^{*}(BG)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that because
<math id="S3.E14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG</annotation></semantics></math> are infinite dimensional, <math id="S3.E14.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> can be non-zero for
<math id="S3.E14.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily large.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.15</span></span><span id="S3.E15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(The localization theorem)
If <math id="S3.E15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G={\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G={\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> we may take <math id="S3.E15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> to be the limit as
<math id="S3.E15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> goes to infinity of the spaces <math id="S3.E15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\smallsetminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\smallsetminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> with
usual free action of <math id="S3.E15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
quotient is the topological space <math id="S3.E15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}{\mathbb{P}}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}{\mathbb{P}}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="S3.E15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{G}^{*}(pt)=H_{G}^{*}(BG)={\mathbb{Z}}[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{G}^{*}(pt)=H_{G}^{*}(BG)={\mathbb{Z}}[t]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.E15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S3.E15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{O}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{O}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Pullback along the projection to a point implies that for any <math id="S3.E15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
space <math id="S3.E15.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
the equivariant cohomology
<math id="S3.E15.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.E15.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}[t]</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Let <math id="S3.E15.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed locus for the
<math id="S3.E15.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-action. The inclusion of the fixed locus <math id="S3.E15.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{T}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{T}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map
in equivariant cohomology <math id="S3.E15.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X)\to H^{*}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X^{T})=H^{*}(X^{T})\otimes{\mathbb{Z}}[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X)\to H^{*}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X^{T})=H^{*}(X^{T})\otimes{\mathbb{Z}}[t]</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S3.E15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">localization theorem</span> states
that this map is an isomorphism after inverting the multiplicative set
of homogenous elements in <math id="S3.E15.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}[t]</annotation></semantics></math> of positive degree. The localization
theorem is very powerful because it reduces certain calculations on <math id="S3.E15.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to calculations on
the fixed locus <math id="S3.E15.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. In many situations the fixed locus of a space
is quite simple - for example a finite number of points.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon</a>]</cite> Kontsevich developed the theory of stable maps and used the
localization theorem (and its corollary, the Bott residue formula)
on the moduli space of stable maps
to give recursive formulas for the number of
rational curves of a given degree on a general quintic hypersurface in <math id="S3.E15.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Subsequently, Graber and Pandharipande <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">GP</a>]</cite>
proved a virtual localization formula for equivariant Chow classes.
Their formula has been one of the
primary tools in algebraic Gromov-Witten theory. For an introduction
to this subject in algebraic geometry see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bri</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The relationship between equivariant cohomology and the cohomology of
a quotient CFG is given by the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.16</span></span><span id="S3.E16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.E16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG where <math id="S3.E16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a
complex variety and <math id="S3.E16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> an algebraic group then <math id="S3.E16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=H^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=H^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2. </span>Proof of theorem Theorem <a href="#S3.E16" title="Theorem 3.16. ‣ 3.2.1. Equivariant cohomology ‣ 3.2. Quotient CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a></h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately even if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic group, the topological spaces <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> used in the construction of
equivariant cohomology are not algebraic varieties. As a result
the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times EG\to X\times_{G}EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times EG\to X\times_{G}EG</annotation></semantics></math> is not an object in the
category <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is difficult to directly compare
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{G}(X):=H^{*}(X\times_{G}EG)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{G}(X):=H^{*}(X\times_{G}EG)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However Totaro
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tot</a>]</cite> observed that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by algebraic varieties.
Precisely,
if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex representation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
containing an open set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\codim V\smallsetminus U&gt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>codim</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\codim V\smallsetminus U&gt;i</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U/G</annotation></semantics></math> approximates <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG</annotation></semantics></math> up to real codimension <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i</annotation></semantics></math>; i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U/G)=H^{k}(BG)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U/G)=H^{k}(BG)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that for every <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a complex representation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\codim V\smallsetminus U&gt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>codim</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\codim V\smallsetminus U&gt;i</annotation></semantics></math>. The reason is that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> may be embedded in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and
such representations are easily constructed for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.17</span></span><span id="S3.E17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.E17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G,U,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G,U,V</annotation></semantics></math> are as in the preceding paragraph then <math id="S3.E17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)=H^{k}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)=H^{k}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.E17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">First observe that if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i+1</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}(U)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}(U)=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is
the complement of a subspace of real codimension at least <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i+2</annotation></semantics></math> in
the contractible space <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>. In
particular, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U)=H^{k}(EG)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U)=H^{k}(EG)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that as a
consequence the quotients <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}EG</annotation></semantics></math> have the
same cohomology up to degree <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this consider the quotient
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=X\times_{G}(U\times EG)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=X\times_{G}(U\times EG)</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a projection <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\colon Z\to X\times_{G}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\colon Z\to X\times_{G}U</annotation></semantics></math> which is an <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math>-fibration and a projection <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}\colon Z\to X\times_{G}EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}\colon Z\to X\times_{G}EG</annotation></semantics></math> which is a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-fibration. Since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(EG)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(EG)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism and since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U)=0</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism in degree <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X\times_{G}EG)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X\times_{G}EG)</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math> as claimed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme then, with mild assumptions on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we may
always find <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> such that the quotient <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}U</annotation></semantics></math>
is also a scheme<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              In particular if <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective or
<math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a>, Proposition 23]</cite>.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In these cases our theorem now follows from the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.18</span></span><span id="S3.E18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With <math id="S3.E18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G,U,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G,U,V</annotation></semantics></math> as above
if <math id="S3.E18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> then the pullback map
<math id="S3.E18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\to H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\to H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism for <math id="S3.E18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon X\times_{G}U\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon X\times_{G}U\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the map
associated to the torsor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times U\to X\times_{G}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times U\to X\times_{G}U</annotation></semantics></math> and equivariant
projection map <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times U\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times U\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> then
the image of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> under the pullback map is the cohomology class
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(u)\in H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(u)\in H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(u)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(u)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We wish to show that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for every object <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponding to a torsor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and equivariant map <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. Given such a torsor consider the cartesian diagram of
torsors.</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}E\times U&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
E\times_{G}U&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}E\times U&amp;\to&amp;E\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
E\times_{G}U&amp;\to&amp;T\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Because the fiber of the map <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{G}U\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{G}U\to E</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>
the map on quotients <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\times_{G}U\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\times_{G}U\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is also a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> fibration. Since
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>, the pullback map <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(T)\to H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(T)\to H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> as an element of
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(u)</annotation></semantics></math> have equal
pullbacks in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(u)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(u)=0</annotation></semantics></math>
it follows that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)=0</annotation></semantics></math> as well. Since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> was arbitrary we see
that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so the map is injective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The fact that the map <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(T)\to H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(T)\to H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism also implies that
our map is surjective. Given a class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(u)\in H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(u)\in H^{k}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math> we
let
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)</annotation></semantics></math> be the inverse image in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> of the pullback of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(u)</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(E\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3. </span>Equivariant homology</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Totaro’s approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> with open sets in representations
allows one to define a corresponding equivariant homology theory which
is dual to equivariant cohomology when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional group and let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be an
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> containing an open set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> on
which <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely and whose complement has codimension greater
than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X-i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.19</span></span><span id="S3.E19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Equivariant homology)<span id="S3.E19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.E19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.E19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-space defined over <math id="S3.E19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and define <math id="S3.E19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}^{G}(X)=H_{i+l-g}^{BM}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}^{G}(X)=H_{i+l-g}^{BM}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.E19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}^{BM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}^{BM}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates Borel-Moore homology
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful3</a>, Section 19.1]</cite> for the definition of Borel-Moore homology).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.20</span></span><span id="S3.E20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Again this definition is independent of the choice of
<math id="S3.E20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> as long as the codimension of <math id="S3.E20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\smallsetminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\smallsetminus U</annotation></semantics></math> is
sufficiently high. The reason we use Borel-Moore homology theory
rather than singular homology is that on a non-compact manifold it
is the theory naturally dual to singular cohomology. In equivariant
theory, even if <math id="S3.E20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact, the quotients <math id="S3.E20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}U</annotation></semantics></math> will in
general not be compact. On compact spaces Borel-Moore homology
coincides with the usual singular homology. Note that our grading
convention means that <math id="S3.E20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> can be non-zero for arbitrarily
negative <math id="S3.E20.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="S3.E20.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}^{G}(X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}^{G}(X)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.E20.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.21</span></span><span id="S3.E21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Equivariant Poincaré duality)<span id="S3.E21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.E21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be smooth <math id="S3.E21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <math id="S3.E21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-space defined over <math id="S3.E21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
map
<math id="S3.E21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{G}(X)\to H_{2n-k}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{G}(X)\to H_{2n-k}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.E21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\mapsto c\cap[X]_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\mapsto c\cap[X]_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Equivariant Chow groups</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When we work over an arbitrary field Totaro’s approximation of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math>
lets us define equivariant Chow groups. The theory of equivariant Chow groups
was developed in the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.22</span></span><span id="S3.E22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a>]</cite>
Let <math id="S3.E22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S3.E22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme defined over an arbitrary field. Then with the
notation as in Definition <a href="#S3.E19" title="Definition 3.19 (Equivariant homology). ‣ 3.2.3. Equivariant homology ‣ 3.2. Quotient CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a> define <math id="S3.E22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{G}(X)=A_{i+l-g}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{G}(X)=A_{i+l-g}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.23</span></span><span id="S3.E23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Again the
definition is independent of the choice of <math id="S3.E23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U,V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E23.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E23.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because equivariant Chow groups are defined as Chow groups of schemes
they enjoy the same formal properties as ordinary Chow groups.
In particular, if <math id="S3.E23.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth then there is an intersection
product on the equivariant Chow groups. Note, however, that cycles may
have negative dimension; i.e. <math id="S3.E23.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> can be non-zero for <math id="S3.E23.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence one cannot conclude that a particular
equivariant intersection product is 0 for dimensional reasons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E23.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E23.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For schemes over <math id="S3.E23.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
the cycle maps <math id="S3.E23.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}(X\times_{G}U)\to H_{2k}^{BM}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}(X\times_{G}U)\to H_{2k}^{BM}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math> of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful3</a>, Chapter 19]</cite>
define cycle maps on equivariant homology <math id="S3.E23.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl\colon A_{k}^{G}(X)\to H_{2k}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl\colon A_{k}^{G}(X)\to H_{2k}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.E23.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of complex dimension <math id="S3.E23.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
the latter group can be identified with <math id="S3.E23.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2n-k}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2n-k}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E23.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E23.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.E23.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.E23.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-space then any <math id="S3.E23.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant subvariety <math id="S3.E23.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S3.E23.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
defines an
equivariant fundamental class <math id="S3.E23.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[V]_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[V]_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E23.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2k}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2k}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.E23.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, unless <math id="S3.E23.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts with finite stabilizers then
<math id="S3.E23.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is not even rationally generated by fundamental classes of invariant
subvarieties.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>Equivariant Chow cohomology</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The definition of equivariant Chow cohomology is analogous to the
definition of
ordinary Chow cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.24</span></span><span id="S3.E24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An equivariant Chow cohomology class <math id="S3.E24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in A^{i}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in A^{i}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an assignment of an operation <math id="S3.E24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)\colon A_{*}^{G}(T)\to A_{*-i}^{G}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)\colon A_{*}^{G}(T)\to A_{*-i}^{G}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> for every equivariant map <math id="S3.E24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to X</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <math id="S3.E24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are
compatible with the usual operations in equivariant intersection theory (flat
pullback and lci pullback, proper pushforward, etc.). Composition
makes the set of equivariant Chow cohomology classes into a graded
ring which we denote <math id="S3.E24.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A^{*}_{G}(X):=\oplus_{i=0}^{\infty}A^{i}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}_{G}(X):=\oplus_{i=0}^{\infty}A^{i}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.25</span></span><span id="S3.E25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because equivariant Chow groups can be non-zero in
arbitrary negative degree <math id="S3.E25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> may be non-zero for arbitrarily
large <math id="S3.E25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.26</span></span><span id="S3.E26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.E26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a><span id="S3.E26.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 19]</span></cite><span id="S3.E26.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If <math id="S3.E26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient CFG then <math id="S3.E26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=A^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})=A^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and an open set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts
freely such that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\codim(V\smallsetminus U)&gt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>codim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\codim(V\smallsetminus U)&gt;i</annotation></semantics></math> then we can compare
the Chow cohomology of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}U</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.27</span></span><span id="S3.E27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.E27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a><span id="S3.E27.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Corollary 2]</span></cite><span id="S3.E27.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> 
If <math id="S3.E27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has an equivariant resolution of singularities then <math id="S3.E27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}_{G}(X)=A^{k}(X\times_{G}U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}_{G}(X)=A^{k}(X\times_{G}U)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.E27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying Proposition <a href="#S3.E27" title="Proposition 3.27. ‣ 3.3.1. Equivariant Chow cohomology ‣ 3.3. Equivariant Chow groups ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.27</span></a> and the Poincaré
duality isomorphism between Chow groups and Chow cohomology we obtain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.28</span></span><span id="S3.E28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.E28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of dimension <math id="S3.E28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
then there is an isomorphism <math id="S3.E28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\to A_{n-k}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\to A_{n-k}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.E28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the ring structure on <math id="S3.E28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> given by
composition of operations is compatible with the ring structure on
<math id="S3.E28.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}^{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}^{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> given by equivariant intersection product.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.29</span></span><span id="S3.E29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E29.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Putting the various propositions together implies that if <math id="S3.E29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth, then a
<math id="S3.E29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant subvariety <math id="S3.E29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> defines a Chow cohomology class
<math id="S3.E29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{V}\in A^{*}([X/G])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{V}\in A^{*}([X/G])</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.E29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts with finite stabilizers then the
classes
<math id="S3.E29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> generate <math id="S3.E29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}([X/G])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}([X/G])</annotation></semantics></math> rationally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a>, Proposition 13]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E29.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E29.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition when <math id="S3.E29.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="S3.E29.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> there is a degree-doubling cycle class map
<math id="S3.E29.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl\colon A^{*}([X/G])\to H^{*}([X/G])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl\colon A^{*}([X/G])\to H^{*}([X/G])</annotation></semantics></math> having the same formal
properties as the cycle class map on smooth complex varieties.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Picard groups of moduli problems</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum1</a>, Section 5]</cite> Mumford defined the integral Picard group of what he
called the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moduli problem</span> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. As Mumford noted, the definition
can be expressed in the language of fibered categories and
makes sense for an arbitrary CFG over <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.30</span></span><span id="S3.E30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum1</a>, p.64]</cite>
Let <math id="S3.E30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a CFG. A line bundle on <math id="S3.E30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
assignment to every scheme <math id="S3.E30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and every object <math id="S3.E30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.E30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>
a line bundle <math id="S3.E30.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(t)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.E30.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. The line bundles <math id="S3.E30.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(t)</annotation></semantics></math> should satisfy the
natural compatibility conditions with respect to pullbacks in the
category <math id="S3.E30.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Tensor product makes the collection of line bundles on <math id="S3.E30.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> into
an abelian group <math id="S3.E30.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a line on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> has a first Chern class
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(L)\in A^{1}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(L)\in A^{1}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.31</span></span><span id="S3.E31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.E31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a><span id="S3.E31.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 18]</span></cite><span id="S3.E31.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S3.E31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> be a quotient CFG with <math id="S3.E31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> smooth. Then
the map <math id="S3.E31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic({\mathcal{X}})\to A^{1}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic({\mathcal{X}})\to A^{1}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.E31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\mapsto c_{1}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\mapsto c_{1}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.32</span></span><span id="S3.E32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E32.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.E32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}=[\overline{H}_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}=[\overline{H}_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth,
the group <math id="S3.E32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> defined here may identified with the group
<math id="S3.E32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{fun}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{fun}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">HM</a>, Definition 3.87]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Equivariant cohomology and the CFG of curves</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results of Sections <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Quotient CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and
<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Equivariant Chow groups ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> imply that the cohomology (resp. Chow) rings of
the CFGs <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are identified with the
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-6}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant cohomology (resp. Chow) rings of the smooth
varieties <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular any invariant subvariety of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an integral class in <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The description of the cohomology of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as the equivariant
cohomology of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> gives, in principal, a method for computing the
integral cohomology ring <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Unfortunately, for <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> there
are no effective methods for carrying out computations. Note that the
rings <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> will have non-zero torsion in
arbitrarily high degree. However, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;6g-6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;6g-6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;3g-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;3g-3</annotation></semantics></math> (Proposition <a href="#S4.E39" title="Proposition 4.39. ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.39</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.33</span></span><span id="S3.E33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The stable cohomology ring of <math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.E33.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E33.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The best results on the cohomology of
<math id="S3.E33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> use the fact that its rational cohomology is the same as the
rational cohomology of the mapping class group. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har</a>]</cite> Harer
used topological methods to prove that <math id="S3.E33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> stabilizes for <math id="S3.E33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus we can define the stable cohomology ring of <math id="S3.E33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as the limit
of the <math id="S3.E33.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.E33.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. In
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">MW</a>]</cite>
Madsen and Weiss proved Mumford’s conjecture: the stable cohomology
ring
of <math id="S3.E33.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.E33.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the ring <math id="S3.E33.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E33.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E33.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the stable cohomology ring of <math id="S3.E33.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.E33.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is
algebraic,
a natural question is the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.34</span></span><span id="S3.E34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E34.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is there a version of Harer’s stability theorem for the Chow groups of
<math id="S3.E34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E33.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E33.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To prove the stability theorem Harer used the fact that there are maps
<math id="S3.E33.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to H^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the topological operation of
cutting out two disks from a
Riemann surface of genus <math id="S3.E33.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and gluing in a “pair of pants” to
obtain
a Riemann surface of genus <math id="S3.E33.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since there is no algebraic counterpart to
this operation, Question <a href="#S3.E34" title="Question 3.34. ‣ Example 3.33 (The stable cohomology ring of 
            
              
                
                M
                g
              
            
          ). ‣ 3.4. Equivariant cohomology and the CFG of curves ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.34</span></a> seems to be out of
reach of current techniques.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.35</span></span><span id="S3.E35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Integral Chow rings of curves of very low genus)<span id="S3.E35.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E35.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In very low genus and for hyperelliptic curves the methods of
equivariant algebraic geometry have been successfully used to compute
integral Chow rings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E35.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E35.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In genus <math id="S3.E35.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> the Chow ring of <math id="S3.E35.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{0}=B\PGL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{0}=B\PGL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> was computed by
Pandharipande
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan</a>]</cite>. The Chow ring of <math id="S3.E35.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> was computed by
Fulghesu and the author <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">EF1</a>]</cite>. The rational Chow ring of
<math id="S3.E35.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}^{\leq 3}</annotation></semantics></math> was computed by Fulghesu in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ful1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E35.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E35.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In genus 1, the Chow rings of <math id="S3.E35.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.E35.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> were
computed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a>]</cite>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vis2</a>]</cite> Vistoli
computed the Chow ring of <math id="S3.E35.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fulghesu, Viviani and the author <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">EF2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">FV</a>]</cite>
extended
Vistoli’s techniques to compute, for all <math id="S3.E35.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, the Chow ring of the CFG
<math id="S3.E35.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
parametrizing hyperelliptic curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E35.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E35.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In each case the presentations of the Chow rings are relatively
simple. The reader may refer to the cited papers for the precise
statements.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In a sense, the greatest value of the equivariant point of view
is psychological. It turns intersection theory on the CFGs <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
into equivariant intersection theory on the smooth varieties <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way we can avoid worrying about the
intricate rational intersection theory on orbifolds and stacks developed by
Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum3</a>]</cite>, Vistoli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vis1</a>]</cite>, and Gillet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gil</a>]</cite>.
In addition, because the intersection theory has integer coefficients, we may
obtain slightly stronger results. We illustrate with a simple example
from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.36</span></span><span id="S3.E36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.E36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following relation holds in the integral Chow ring <math id="S3.E36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\lambda_{1}+\ldots+\lambda_{g})(1-\lambda_{1}+\ldots+(-1)^{g}\lambda_{g})=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\lambda_{1}+\ldots+\lambda_{g})(1-\lambda_{1}+\ldots+(-1)^{g}\lambda_{g})=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply the argument of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum3</a>]</cite> pp. 306 to the family of
tri-canonically smooth curves <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{g}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{g}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to
conclude
that <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c({\mathbb{E}}_{H_{g}})c(\check{{\mathbb{E}}}_{H_{g}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c({\mathbb{E}}_{H_{g}})c(\check{{\mathbb{E}}}_{H_{g}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}_{H_{g}}=\pi_{*}(\omega_{Z_{g}/H_{g}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}_{H_{g}}=\pi_{*}(\omega_{Z_{g}/H_{g}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the
Hodge bundle on <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under the identification <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})=A^{*}_{G}(H_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})=A^{*}_{G}(H_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> the tautological
class
<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> identifies with <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}({\mathbb{E}}_{H_{g}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}({\mathbb{E}}_{H_{g}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Mumford proved a number of other relations using the
Grothendieck-Riemann-Roch theorem. Again these can be derived using
the equivariant version of the Grothendieck-Riemann-Roch theorem for
the map <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Z}_{g}\to\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Z}_{g}\to\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the formulas for the Chern
character involves denominators, Mumford’s formulas hold
after inverting the primes dividing the denominators. A natural
question is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.E38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.38</span></span><span id="S3.E38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E38.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Do the tautological relations obtained by Mumford in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum3</a>]</cite>
hold in <math id="S3.E38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
or
<math id="S3.E38.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> after minimally clearing denominators? Similarly, do Faber’s relations
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fab</a>]</cite> hold after minimally clearing denominators?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.E39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.E39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.39</span></span><span id="S3.E39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.E39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.E39.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The author does not have any particular insight into this
question. However, we point out that Mumford proved
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum2</a>, Theorem 5.10]</cite>
that the relation</p>
<table id="S3.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\lambda_{1}=\kappa_{1}+\delta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\lambda_{1}=\kappa_{1}+\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.E39.p1.2" class="ltx_p">holds integrally in <math id="S3.E39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pic(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})=A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pic(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})=A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.E39.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\sum_{i=0}^{[g/2]}\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\sum_{i=0}^{[g/2]}\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Mumford
was able to show this because he used transcendental methods to prove
that <math id="S3.E39.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic^{G}(\overline{H}_{g})=\Pic(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})=A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic^{G}(\overline{H}_{g})=\Pic(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})=A^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is
torsion free.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of this final section is to define Deligne-Mumford
stacks and their moduli spaces. As mentioned in the introduction,
stacks are CFGs with certain algebro-geometric properties. The CFGs
<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are all
Deligne-Mumford stacks whenever <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Associated to a Deligne-Mumford stack is a <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coarse moduli space</span>. In
general the coarse moduli space of a Deligne-Mumford stack is
an algebraic space, but the moduli
spaces of the stacks of smooth and stable curves are
in fact quasi-projective and projective varieties. In Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> we
will construct an isomorphism between the rational Chow groups
of a quotient Deligne-Mumford stack and the rational Chow groups of
its moduli space. This isomorphism defines an intersection product on
the Chow groups of the moduli space. A similar result also holds for
homology.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Deligne-Mumford stacks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far we have not imposed any algebro-geometric conditions on our
CFGs. A Deligne-Mumford stack is a CFG which is, in an appropriate
sense, “locally” a scheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.E1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a CFG <math id="S4.E1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> choose for each morphism of
scheme <math id="S4.E1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> and each object <math id="S4.E1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>
an object <math id="S4.E1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> such that there is a morphism
<math id="S4.E1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t\to t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t\to t</annotation></semantics></math> whose image in <math id="S4.E1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the map <math id="S4.E1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. The
axioms
of a CFG imply that the <math id="S4.E1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t</annotation></semantics></math> always exist and are unique up to canonical
isomorphism.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.E2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.E2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a CFG. Let <math id="S4.E2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an
<math id="S4.E2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and let <math id="S4.E2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{T_{i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{i}}}{{\to}}T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{i}}}{{\to}}T\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection
of étale maps whose images cover <math id="S4.E2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S4.E2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}=T_{i}\times_{T}T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}=T_{i}\times_{T}T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and identify <math id="S4.E2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}=T_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}=T_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.E2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly denote by <math id="S4.E2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ijk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ijk}</annotation></semantics></math> any of the products canonically
isomorphic
to <math id="S4.E2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}\times_{T_{j}}T_{jk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}\times_{T_{j}}T_{jk}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.E2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an object
<math id="S4.E2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S4.E2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> be the pullback of <math id="S4.E2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S4.E2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> along the projection map <math id="S4.E2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}\to T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}\to T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <span id="S4.E2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">descent datum</span> for the covering <math id="S4.E2.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{T_{i}\to T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{i}\to T\}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a collection of objects <math id="S4.E2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
together with isomorphisms <math id="S4.E2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{ij}\colon t_{ij}\to t_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{ij}\colon t_{ij}\to t_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S4.E2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfy the cocycle condition when pulled
back
to <math id="S4.E2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ijk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ijk})</annotation></semantics></math>; i.e., <math id="S4.E2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{jk}\circ\phi_{ij}=\phi_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{jk}\circ\phi_{ij}=\phi_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math>
after pullback to <math id="S4.E2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ijk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ijk})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.E3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>, Definition 4.6]</cite>
A CFG <math id="S4.E3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.E3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S4.E3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deligne-Mumford (DM) stack</span> if the following 3 conditions
are satisfied.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">i) The diagonal map <math id="S4.E3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times_{S}{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times_{S}{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable, quasi-compact and and
separated.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              The representability of the diagonal map is
equivalent to the condition that any morphism from a scheme <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vis1</a>, Proposition 7.13]</cite>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">ii) (Effectivity of descent)
If <math id="S4.E3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.E3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and <math id="S4.E3.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{T_{i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{i}}}{{\to}}T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{i}}}{{\to}}T\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an étale cover of <math id="S4.E3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
then given descent datum relative to this covering there exists an
object
<math id="S4.E3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and isomorphisms <math id="S4.E3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}\colon p_{i}^{*}t\to t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}\colon p_{i}^{*}t\to t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
such that after pullback to <math id="S4.E3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.E3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{ij}=\phi_{j}\circ\phi_{i}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{ij}=\phi_{j}\circ\phi_{i}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">iii) There exists an étale surjective morphism from a scheme <math id="S4.E3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(The representability of the diagonal implies that any <math id="S4.E3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable, so it makes sense to talk about this
map being étale and surjective.)</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.E4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>]</cite> the term algebraic stack was used for a CFG
satisfying the conditions of Definition <a href="#S4.E3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. However,
recent works reserve the term <span id="S4.E4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraic stack</span> for a more general
class of stacks defined by Artin (also called Artin stacks). Note
that we do not define a <span id="S4.E4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stack</span> - only a <span id="S4.E4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deligne-Mumford stack</span>.
A stack is a generalization to CFGs of the concept of an étale
sheaf. The definition of a stack is given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>, Definition
4.1]</cite>.
A CFG satisfying conditions i) and ii) of Definition <a href="#S4.E3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>
is a stack in the sense of Definition 4.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>]</cite> and all
stacks satisfy condition (ii) of our definition. However, not all
stacks satisfy condition
(i).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.E5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.E5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a CFG associated to a functor i) and ii) imply
that <math id="S4.E5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sheaf in the étale topology. A
Deligne-Mumford stack which is a sheaf is called an <span id="S4.E5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraic
space</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>The diagonal and automorphisms</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To give a morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
equivalent to giving a pair of objects <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a CFG with a representable
diagonal then the
fiber product <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is the scheme which represents the functor
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{t_{1},t_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{t_{1},t_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This functor assigns to any
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> the set of isomorphisms in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Note that for
any CFG and any scheme <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber product <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is automatically the CFG
associated to the functor <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{t_{1},t_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{t_{1},t_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">LMB</a>, Lemma 2.4.1.4]</cite>.)
In particular, if <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}=t_{2}=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}=t_{2}=t</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is the
scheme
which represents the automorphism group-scheme <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(t)\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(t)\to T</annotation></semantics></math> whose
fiber
over a point <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon\Spec k\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon\Spec k\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is the automorphism group of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The condition that there is an étale surjective morphism from a
scheme <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the these automorphism groups
are finite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.E6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.E6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vis1</a><span id="S4.E6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 7.15]</span></cite><span id="S4.E6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If <math id="S4.E6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack then the diagonal <math id="S4.E6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unramified. Equivalently, for
every algebraically closed field <math id="S4.E6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and every object <math id="S4.E6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)</annotation></semantics></math>
the automorphism group <math id="S4.E6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and reduced over <math id="S4.E6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Quotient CFGs and Deligne-Mumford stacks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a scheme of finite type over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an smooth affine group scheme over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> then it is
relatively straightforward to show that the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the effective
descent condition of Definition <a href="#S4.E3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and the diagonal of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is representable, quasi-compact and separated. The only
question is whether there exists an étale surjective morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is étale over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (for example if <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is
a point and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is finite) then the map <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is étale so <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack. Unfortunately, CFGs
such as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are quotients by non-finite groups.
The next theorem gives a criterion for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> to be a DM stack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.E7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.E7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth group scheme over <math id="S4.E7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. A quotient CFG
<math id="S4.E7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack if and only if the stabilizer of
every geometric point of <math id="S4.E7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and reduced.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.E8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over a field of characteristic 0 every group scheme is smooth, so the
the stabilizers of geometric points are always reduced. In this case
<math id="S4.E8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack if and only if <math id="S4.E8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.E8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with finite
stabilizers.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> be a quotient CFG and
let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be an
object of
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraically closed field. Since any torsor <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to\Spec K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to\Spec K</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial
the isomorphism class of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a
single <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit the image of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an orbit. Hence objects
of
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits. The automorphism
group of an object of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(\Spec K)</annotation></semantics></math> is the just the
stabilizer of that orbit. Hence, a necessary condition for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> to
be a DM stack is that the stabilizer of every point is finite and
reduced.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The converse follows from the following theorem, originally stated by Deligne and
Mumford.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.E9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.E9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a><span id="S4.E9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Thm 4.21]</span></cite><span id="S4.E9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S4.E9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a CFG which satisfies conditions i) and ii) of
Definition <a href="#S4.E3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Assume in addition that</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E9.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i’) The diagonal <math id="S4.E9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is unramified.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E9.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii’) There exists a smooth surjective map from a scheme <math id="S4.E9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.E9.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E9.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then </span><math id="S4.E9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.E9.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a DM stack.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.E10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In characteristic 0 there is a
relatively simple proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.E9" title="Theorem 4.9. ‣ Proof sketch. ‣ 4.1.2. Quotient CFGs and Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> when <math id="S4.E10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is
reductive, <math id="S4.E10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth (or even normal) and covered by <math id="S4.E10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant affine open
sets. The proof goes as follows. If <math id="S4.E10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a point of <math id="S4.E10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
then <math id="S4.E10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is an contained in an affine <math id="S4.E10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open set
<math id="S4.E10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conditions i) and ii) imply that the stabilizers of the <math id="S4.E10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action
are finite so the <math id="S4.E10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of closed points are also closed.
The étale slice theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lun</a>]</cite> then implies that for closed points
there is a smooth
affine variety <math id="S4.E10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant strongly étale
map<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                  
                  
                  
                A <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map of affine varieties <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec A\to\Spec B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec A\to\Spec B</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strongly étale</span> if the induced map <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec A^{G}\to\Spec B^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec A^{G}\to\Spec B^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is étale and <math id="footnote5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B\otimes_{B^{G}}A^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B\otimes_{B^{G}}A^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. These
conditions imply that the map <math id="footnote5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec A\to\Spec B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec A\to\Spec B</annotation></semantics></math> is also étale.</span></span></span>
<math id="S4.E10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times_{G_{x}}W_{x}\to U_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times_{G_{x}}W_{x}\to U_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> (Here <math id="S4.E10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stabilizer of <math id="S4.E10.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>). The collection of <math id="S4.E10.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W_{x}\}_{x\in X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W_{x}\}_{x\in X}</annotation></semantics></math> forms an étale cover of
<math id="S4.E10.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>CFGs of stable curves are DM stacks</h4>

<div id="S4.E11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.E11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The CFGs <math id="S4.E11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.E11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are DM stacks if <math id="S4.E11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}=[\overline{H}_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}=[\overline{H}_{g}/\PGL_{5g-5}]</annotation></semantics></math> we know that it satisfies
conditions i) and ii) of Definition <a href="#S4.E3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> as well as
condition ii’) of Theorem <a href="#S4.E9" title="Theorem 4.9. ‣ Proof sketch. ‣ 4.1.2. Quotient CFGs and Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a>. The only thing to show
is that the diagonal is unramified. This is equivalent to showing that
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and unramified for every stable curve <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
defined
over an algebraically closed field <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. The last fact follows from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>, Lemma 1.4]</cite> which implies that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> does not have any
infinitesimal automorphisms.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A similar argument shows that the CFG <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack as
well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.E12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.E12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> then the CFGs <math id="S4.E12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are DM
stacks.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by
Knudsen’s theorem. The proof follows by induction and the following
simple lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.13</span></span><span id="S4.E13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.E13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a representable morphism of
CFGs. If <math id="S4.E13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack then <math id="S4.E13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is as well.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.14</span></span><span id="S4.E14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Curves of very low genus)
The CFGs <math id="S4.E14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are not DM stacks since the automorphism
group of <math id="S4.E14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.E14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the automorphism group of a curve
<math id="S4.E14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>
of genus 1 contains the curve itself.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4. </span>Separated and proper DM stacks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly discuss what it means for a DM stack to be separated
or proper over the ground scheme <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. There is also a notion of
separation and properness for arbitrary morphisms of DM stacks but we
do not discuss this here. Note that we have already implicitly defined
what it means for a representable morphism to be separated or proper,
since these properties are preserved by base change
(Remark <a href="#S2.E29" title="Remark 2.29. ‣ 2.4.1. Fiber products of CFGs ‣ 2.4. Fiber products of CFGs and universal curves ‣ 2. Categories fibred in groupoids (CFGs) ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.29</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.15</span></span><span id="S4.E15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A DM stack <math id="S4.E15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.E15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">separated</span> over <math id="S4.E15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> if the diagonal
<math id="S4.E15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times_{S}{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}\times_{S}{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
proper.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.16</span></span><span id="S4.E16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the diagonal of a DM stack is unramified, a DM stack is
separated if and only if the diagonal is finite.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Not surprisingly for quotient DM stacks the separation condition can be
characterized in terms of the group action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.17</span></span><span id="S4.E17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A quotient stack <math id="S4.E17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is separated if and only if <math id="S4.E17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
acts properly on <math id="S4.E17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; i.e. the map <math id="S4.E17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times X\to X\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times X\to X\times X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.E17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,x)\mapsto(x,gx)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,x)\mapsto(x,gx)</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper morphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following result from GIT shows that the properness of a group
action is a relatively natural condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.18</span></span><span id="S4.E18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.E18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MFK</a><span id="S4.E18.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Converse 1.13]</span></cite><span id="S4.E18.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S4.E18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> act on a projective variety and assume that the generic
stabilizer is finite. Let <math id="S4.E18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{s}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{s}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of stable points with
respect to an ample line bundle <math id="S4.E18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.E18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
acts properly on <math id="S4.E18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{s}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{s}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To define the notion of a stack being proper over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> we invoke the
following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.19</span></span><span id="S4.E19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.E19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">EHKV</a><span id="S4.E19.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 2.7]</span></cite><span id="S4.E19.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If <math id="S4.E19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack then there exits a finite surjective
morphism from a scheme <math id="S4.E19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.20</span></span><span id="S4.E20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.E20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient stack then Theorem
<a href="#S4.E19" title="Theorem 4.19. ‣ 4.1.4. Separated and proper DM stacks ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.19</span></a> implies that there exists a finite surjective
map <math id="S4.E20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.E20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on <math id="S4.E20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S4.E20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor. This consequence was originally proved by Seshadri
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ses</a>, Theorem 6.1]</cite>
and the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.E19" title="Theorem 4.19. ‣ 4.1.4. Separated and proper DM stacks ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.19</span></a> is an adaptation of
Seshadri’s argument to stacks.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.21</span></span><span id="S4.E21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A separated DM stack <math id="S4.E21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.E21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proper</span> over <math id="S4.E21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> if there
exists a finite surjective morphism from a scheme <math id="S4.E21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S4.E21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> proper over <math id="S4.E21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.22</span></span><span id="S4.E22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As is the case for morphisms of schemes there are valuative criteria
for separation and properness of morphisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>, Theorem
4.18, 4.19]</cite>. We do not state them here, but Deligne and
Mumford used the valuative criteria together with the stable reduction
theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DM</a>, Corollary 2.7]</cite> to prove the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.23</span></span><span id="S4.E23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The DM stack <math id="S4.E23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper over <math id="S4.E23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The coarse moduli space of a DM stack is a space whose geometric
points correspond to isomorphism classes of objects over the
corresponding algebraically closed field. Before we give a definition
we feel obliged to issue a warning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_warning">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Warning 4.24</span></span><span id="S4.E24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a stack <math id="S4.E24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> such as <math id="S4.E24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> one can define the <span id="S4.E24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coarse moduli functor</span>. This is the contravariant functor <math id="S4.E24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathcal{X}}\colon{\mathcal{S}}\to\Sets" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathcal{X}}\colon{\mathcal{S}}\to\Sets</annotation></semantics></math> which assigns to any scheme <math id="S4.E24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the set of
isomorphism classes in the category <math id="S4.E24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S4.E24.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The coarse moduli space of <math id="S4.E24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not represent the coarse moduli functor
<math id="S4.E24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> For one thing the functor <math id="S4.E24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not in general
a sheaf in étale topology. The reason is that
two objects of a stack may become
isomorphic after base change to an étale cover. For example, a trivial and
iso-trivial family of curves are not isomorphic but become so after
étale base change. For <math id="S4.E24.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be represented by a
scheme or algebraic space it would have to be an étale sheaf. One
may attempt to replace <math id="S4.E24.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> by its
associated sheaf in the étale topology. However, the associated sheaf
need not have an étale cover by a scheme.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.25</span></span><span id="S4.E25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.E25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a separated DM stack. A scheme <math id="S4.E25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a coarse
moduli scheme for <math id="S4.E25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> if there is a map <math id="S4.E25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E25.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E25.p2.1" class="ltx_p">i) <math id="S4.E25.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is universal for maps from <math id="S4.E25.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> to schemes; i.e.. given
a morphism <math id="S4.E25.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.E25.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> a scheme, there is a unique
morphism <math id="S4.E25.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon M\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon M\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.E25.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ p=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ p=q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E25.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E25.p3.1" class="ltx_p">ii) If <math id="S4.E25.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraically closed field then
there is a bijection between the
points of <math id="S4.E25.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(K)</annotation></semantics></math> and the set of isomorphism classes of objects in the
groupoid <math id="S4.E25.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.26</span></span><span id="S4.E26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.E26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be an algebraic group acting on a scheme <math id="S4.E26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S4.E26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a geometric quotient in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MFK</a>, Definition
0.6]</cite> then <math id="S4.E26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a coarse moduli scheme for <math id="S4.E26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition, geometric points of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to isomorphism
classes of objects in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> condition ii) is satisfied.
To give a map <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/G]\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/G]\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to giving a <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant
map <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MFK</a>, Proposition 0.6]</cite> a geometric quotient
is a categorical quotient which implies that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies condition
i).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general a DM stack need not have a coarse moduli scheme, but the
universal property guarantees that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is unique if it exists.
Keel and Mori <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM</a>]</cite> proved that every Artin stack
with finite stabilizer
<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            An algebraic stack <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
has <span id="footnote6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">finite stabilizer</span> if the representable morphism
<math id="footnote6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, where <math id="footnote6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathcal{X}}={\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathcal{X}}={\mathcal{X}}\times_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}}{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the inertia stack. For quotient stacks this condition is equivalent
to the requirement that the map <math id="footnote6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}(X)\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}(X)\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is finite where <math id="footnote6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}(X)=\{(g,x)|gx=x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}(X)=\{(g,x)|gx=x\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Any separated stack has finite stabilizer but
not every stack with finite stabilizer is separated.</span></span></span>
has a coarse moduli space in the category
of algebraic spaces. Their result implies the following theorem for
Deligne-Mumford stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.27</span></span><span id="S4.E27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.E27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Con</a><span id="S4.E27.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S4.E27.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Given a separated DM stack <math id="S4.E27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> there
exists
a separated algebraic space <math id="S4.E27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and a map <math id="S4.E27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E27.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E27.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E27.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i) for every algebraically closed field <math id="S4.E27.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.E27.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> induces a bijection
between the set <math id="S4.E27.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(K)</annotation></semantics></math> and the set of isomorphism classes in <math id="S4.E27.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(K)</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E27.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E27.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E27.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii) <math id="S4.E27.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is universal for maps to algebraic spaces.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E27.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E27.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E27.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if <math id="S4.E27.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper over <math id="S4.E27.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> then so is <math id="S4.E27.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.28</span></span><span id="S4.E28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E28.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The algebraic space <math id="S4.E28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the separated DM stack <math id="S4.E28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a coarse moduli space. Again, the universal property
implies that <math id="S4.E28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is unique up to isomorphism.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since the universal property for maps to algebraic spaces is a stronger
than the universal property for maps to schemes, it is a priori possible for a
DM stack to have an algebraic space as a coarse moduli space and a
different scheme as a coarse moduli scheme. Fortunately, this
does not occur for separated DM stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.29</span></span><span id="S4.E29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM</a>, Proposition 9.1]</cite>)
If <math id="S4.E29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a coarse moduli scheme for a separated DM stack <math id="S4.E29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
then <math id="S4.E29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a coarse moduli space.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.30</span></span><span id="S4.E30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Pathologies of non-separated stacks)<span id="S4.E30.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E30.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we relax the separation hypothesis then a DM stack may have a
coarse moduli space which is not isomorphic to its coarse moduli
scheme.
This is essentially
the phenomenon of Example 0.4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">MFK</a>]</cite>. In that example
Mumford shows that <math id="S4.E30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the geometric quotient by <math id="S4.E30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of an
open set <math id="S4.E30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset{\mathbb{A}}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset{\mathbb{A}}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The action of <math id="S4.E30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in such a way that it is free but not proper. In this case
<math id="S4.E30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the coarse moduli scheme of the non-separated stack
<math id="S4.E30.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/\SL_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/\SL_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the action is free, the stack <math id="S4.E30.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X/\SL_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X/\SL_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> is in
fact a non-separated algebraic space and so is its own coarse moduli space. By
restricting our focus to <span id="S4.E30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">separated</span> stacks we avoid this
difficulty.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1. </span>Singularities of coarse moduli spaces</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is well known that coarse moduli spaces of smooth stacks,
like <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
have quotient singularities. Using the language of stacks we
can make this precise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.31</span></span><span id="S4.E31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.E31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a DM stack with coarse moduli space <math id="S4.E31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
for every point <math id="S4.E31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.E31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> there is an affine scheme <math id="S4.E31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, a finite
group <math id="S4.E31.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, a representable étale morphism <math id="S4.E31.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[U/H]\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[U/H]\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and a cartesian
diagram</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}[U/H]&amp;\to&amp;{\mathcal{X}}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
U/H&amp;\to&amp;M\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>M</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}[U/H]&amp;\to&amp;{\mathcal{X}}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
U/H&amp;\to&amp;M\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.E31.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E31.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that the image of <math id="S4.E31.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U/H</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E31.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S4.E31.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.32</span></span><span id="S4.E32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E32.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The result stated here is a special case of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM</a>, Proposition
4.2]</cite>.
In characteristic 0 if <math id="S4.E32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.E32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> smooth, <math id="S4.E32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> reductive and <math id="S4.E32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
covered by
<math id="S4.E32.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant affine open sets then Theorem <a href="#S4.E31" title="Theorem 4.31. ‣ 4.2.1. Singularities of coarse moduli spaces ‣ 4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a> follows
from the étale slice theorem (cf. Remark
<a href="#S4.E10" title="Remark 4.10. ‣ 4.1.2. Quotient CFGs and Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A corollary of Theorem <a href="#S4.E31" title="Theorem 4.31. ‣ 4.2.1. Singularities of coarse moduli spaces ‣ 4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a> is the converse to
Proposition <a href="#S4.E26" title="Proposition 4.26. ‣ 4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.26</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.33</span></span><span id="S4.E33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.E33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient DM and
<math id="S4.E33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a coarse moduli scheme then <math id="S4.E33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a geometric
quotient of <math id="S4.E33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by the action of <math id="S4.E33.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span>Moduli spaces of curves</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following is well known result was originally proved by Mumford and
Knudsen.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.34</span></span><span id="S4.E34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E34.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.E34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then the DM stack <math id="S4.E34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a coarse moduli scheme <math id="S4.E34.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> which is
a projective variety.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since Knudsen proved that the contraction morphisms <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are representable and projective we
can use induction to obtain the following Corollary of Theorem <a href="#S4.E34" title="Theorem 4.34. ‣ 4.2.2. Moduli spaces of curves ‣ 4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.34</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.35</span></span><span id="S4.E35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.E35.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> the DM stack <math id="S4.E35.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> stack has a projective coarse moduli scheme <math id="S4.E35.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of theorem <a href="#S4.E34" title="Theorem 4.34. ‣ 4.2.2. Moduli spaces of curves ‣ 4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.34</span></a> is based on showing
that a geometric quotient of the quasi-projective variety
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{5g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{5g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> exists as a projective variety. The main
technique is Mumford’s geometric invariant
theory. However, the proof is indirect and also uses techniques developed
by Gieseker on asymptotic stability.
Chapter
4 of the book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">HM</a>]</cite> gives an excellent exposition of the
proof of the projectivity of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this final section we compare the cohomology/Chow ring of a
quotient DM
stack with the cohomology/Chow ring of its moduli space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.36</span></span><span id="S4.E36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E36.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.E36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a DM stack then we define <math id="S4.E36.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S4.E36.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim U</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.E36.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is any étale surjective map from a
scheme.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An immediate consequence of Theorem <a href="#S4.E31" title="Theorem 4.31. ‣ 4.2.1. Singularities of coarse moduli spaces ‣ 4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a> is the
following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.37</span></span><span id="S4.E37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E37.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E37.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.E37.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a separated DM stack and <math id="S4.E37.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is
a coarse moduli scheme, then <math id="S4.E37.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=\dim M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=\dim M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For quotient stacks we can compute the dimension equivariantly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.38</span></span><span id="S4.E38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E38.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E38.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.E38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient DM stack then <math id="S4.E38.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathcal{X}}=\dim X-\dim G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathcal{X}}=\dim X-\dim G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology and Chow cohomology groups of CFG may be non-zero in
arbitrarily high degree. The next results shows that the
rational cohomology groups of a DM stack vanish in degree more than
the real
dimension
of the stack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.39</span></span><span id="S4.E39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E39.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E39.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.E39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a DM stack.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E39.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E39.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E39.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i) If <math id="S4.E39.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="S4.E39.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.E39.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.E39.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;2\dim_{\mathbb{C}}{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;2\dim_{\mathbb{C}}{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E39.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E39.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E39.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii) If <math id="S4.E39.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over an arbitrary field,
<math id="S4.E39.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.E39.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;\dim{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;\dim{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in H^{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;2\dim{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;2\dim{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will show that for any scheme <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and map <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponding to an object <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology
class <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.
Let <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z}}{{\to}}{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>z</mi></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z}}{{\to}}{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite surjective map from a scheme
and let <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}=Z\times_{\mathcal{X}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}=Z\times_{\mathcal{X}}T</annotation></semantics></math> so we have a 2-cartesian diagram</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}Z_{T}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z^{\prime}}}{{\to}}&amp;T\\
t^{\prime}\downarrow&amp;&amp;t\downarrow\\
Z&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{X}}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>T</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>Z</mi></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>z</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}Z_{T}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z^{\prime}}}{{\to}}&amp;T\\
t^{\prime}\downarrow&amp;&amp;t\downarrow\\
Z&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z}}{{\to}}&amp;{\mathcal{X}}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z=\dim{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z=\dim{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> we know that <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other
hand, functoriality implies that <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime*}c(t)=t^{\prime*}c(z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime*}c(t)=t^{\prime*}c(z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now
<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z^{\prime}}}{{\to}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle z^{\prime}}}{{\to}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite surjective morphism of
schemes. Hence it is a ramified covering of the underlying topological
spaces. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smi</a>]</cite> there is a
transfer map <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}_{*}\colon H^{*}(Z_{T},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{*}(T,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}_{*}\colon H^{*}(Z_{T},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{*}(T,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}_{*}z^{\prime*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}_{*}z^{\prime*}</annotation></semantics></math> is
multiplication by the degree of <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)</annotation></semantics></math>
is 0.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof for Chow rings is similar.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For quotient DM stacks with a coarse moduli scheme we obtain a sharper result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E40" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.40</span></span><span id="S4.E40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E40.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E40.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E40.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.E40.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> be a DM quotient stack and let
<math id="S4.E40.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon{\mathcal{X}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> be a coarse moduli scheme.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E40.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E40.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E40.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i) If <math id="S4.E40.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="S4.E40.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> then there are isomorphisms
<math id="S4.E40.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to H^{*}(M,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to H^{*}(M,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.E40.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to H_{*}(M,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to H_{*}(M,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E40.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E40.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E40.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii) In the algebraic category there analogous isomorphisms
<math id="S4.E40.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A^{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A^{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E40.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}({\mathcal{X}})\to A_{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}({\mathcal{X}})\to A_{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof in cohomology is very simple. The coarse moduli space <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
is topologically the space of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/G</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon X\to X/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon X\to X/G</annotation></semantics></math> be the quotient map. There is a map of quotients
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{G}EG\to X/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{G}EG\to X/G</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber at a point <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in X/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in X/G</annotation></semantics></math> is the
quotient <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG/G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG/G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stabilizer of any point in the orbit
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{-1}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{-1}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic the fiber
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EG/G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EG/G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic. Hence the pullback <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(X/G,{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{*}(X\times_{G}EG,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(X/G,{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{*}(X\times_{G}EG,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof in intersection theory is more difficult. It makes use of
the fact that there exists a finite surjective morphism form a scheme
<math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof is given in Section 4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a>]</cite> and the
results is valid even if <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic space.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">As a corollary of Theorem <a href="#S4.E40" title="Theorem 4.40. ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.40</span></a> and equivariant Poincaré
duality (Theorem <a href="#S3.E21" title="Theorem 3.21 (Equivariant Poincaré duality). ‣ 3.2.3. Equivariant homology ‣ 3.2. Quotient CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>)
we conclude that there is an intersection product on the
rational homology groups of the moduli space of a smooth DM quotient
stack.
Similarly, Proposition <a href="#S3.E28" title="Proposition 3.28. ‣ 3.3.1. Equivariant Chow cohomology ‣ 3.3. Equivariant Chow groups ‣ 3. Cohomology of CFGs and equivariant cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.28</span></a> implies
that the rational Chow groups of the
moduli space have an intersection product.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Algebraic cycles on DM stacks and their moduli</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> be a separated quotient DM stack with
coarse moduli scheme <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The isomorphism <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#S4.E40" title="Theorem 4.40. ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.40</span></a> can be explicitly
described using equivariant cycles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E41" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E41.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.41</span></span><span id="S4.E41.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E41.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E41.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.E41.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a><span id="S4.E41.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 13a]</span></cite><span id="S4.E41.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S4.E41.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> be a quotient DM stack. Every element of
<math id="S4.E41.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=A_{*}^{G}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}=A_{*}^{G}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
can written as a <math id="S4.E41.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear combination <math id="S4.E41.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}\alpha_{i}[V_{i}]_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}\alpha_{i}[V_{i}]_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>
where the <math id="S4.E41.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[V_{i}]_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[V_{i}]_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are the fundamental classes of <math id="S4.E41.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant
subvarieties of dimension <math id="S4.E41.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+\dim G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+\dim G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The next result follows from the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG1</a>, Theorem 3a]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E42" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E42.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.42</span></span><span id="S4.E42.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E42.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E42.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.E42.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.E42.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=[X/G]</annotation></semantics></math> be a quotient DM stack defined over a field of
characteristic 0. Suppose that <math id="S4.E42.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a coarse moduli
scheme
corresponding to a geometric quotient
<math id="S4.E42.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon X\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon X\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.E42.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.E42.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subvariety of <math id="S4.E42.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S4.E42.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=p^{-1}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=p^{-1}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.E42.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the order of the stabilizer at a general
point of <math id="S4.E42.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then
the isomorphism <math id="S4.E42.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{k}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of Theorem <a href="#S4.E40" title="Theorem 4.40. ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.40</span></a> maps <math id="S4.E42.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.E42.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{V}[V]_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{V}[V]_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E43" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E43.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.43</span></span><span id="S4.E43.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E43.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E43.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.E43.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
then the map of Proposition <a href="#S4.E42" title="Proposition 4.42. ‣ 4.3.1. Algebraic cycles on DM stacks and their moduli ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.42</span></a>
identifies with the map
the map <math id="S4.E43.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to\Pic_{fun}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to\Pic_{fun}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by Proposition 3.88 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">HM</a>]</cite><span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
                  
                  
                  
                Note that in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">HM</a>]</cite> <math id="footnote7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the coarse moduli scheme while here
<math id="footnote7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the stack of stable curves.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E44" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E44.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.44</span></span><span id="S4.E44.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E44.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E44.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The factor <math id="S4.E44.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> in the statement of Proposition <a href="#S4.E42" title="Proposition 4.42. ‣ 4.3.1. Algebraic cycles on DM stacks and their moduli ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.42</span></a> can
be understood as follows. Let <math id="S4.E44.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a variety on which <math id="S4.E44.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts
properly
and let <math id="S4.E44.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon V\to W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon V\to W</annotation></semantics></math> be a geometric quotient.
By Theorem <a href="#S4.E19" title="Theorem 4.19. ‣ 4.1.4. Separated and proper DM stacks ‣ 4.1. Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.19</span></a> there is finite surjective map
<math id="S4.E44.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon V^{\prime}\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon V^{\prime}\to V</annotation></semantics></math> and a torsor <math id="S4.E44.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}\colon V^{\prime}\to W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}\colon V^{\prime}\to W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to show that
there is an induced map on quotients <math id="S4.E44.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle h}}{{\to}}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mover><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle h}}{{\to}}W</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the following diagram is commutative (but not cartesian).</p>
<table id="S4.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}V^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;\ V\\
\pi^{\prime}\downarrow&amp;&amp;\pi\downarrow\\
W^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle h}}{{\to}}&amp;W\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>V</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>W</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}V^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f}}{{\to}}&amp;\ V\\
\pi^{\prime}\downarrow&amp;&amp;\pi\downarrow\\
W^{\prime}&amp;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle h}}{{\to}}&amp;W\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.E44.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.E44.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E44.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\pi^{-1}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\pi^{-1}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.E44.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> can be
identified
with the orbit of <math id="S4.E44.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> which is isomorphic to the quotient <math id="S4.E44.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G/G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G/G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. On
the other hand, since <math id="S4.E44.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on <math id="S4.E44.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the fibers of <math id="S4.E44.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are
all isomorphic to <math id="S4.E44.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from this observation
that <math id="S4.E44.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg f=e_{V}\deg h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg f=e_{V}\deg h</annotation></semantics></math>. By mapping <math id="S4.E44.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.E44.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{V}[V]_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{V}[V]_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>
we ensure that our map <math id="S4.E44.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{*}^{G}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{*}^{G}(X)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
commutes with proper pushforwards in diagrams such as
(<a href="#S4.E45" title="In Remark 4.44. ‣ 4.3.1. Algebraic cycles on DM stacks and their moduli ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.45</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E44.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E44.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Diagram (<a href="#S4.E45" title="In Remark 4.44. ‣ 4.3.1. Algebraic cycles on DM stacks and their moduli ‣ 4.3. Cohomology of Deligne-Mumford stacks and their moduli spaces ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.45</span></a>) can also be reinterpreted in the
language of stacks as saying the we have a sequence of finite
surjective maps
<math id="S4.E44.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p}}{{\to}}[V/G]\stackrel{{\scriptstyle q}}{{\to}}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>q</mi></mover><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p}}{{\to}}[V/G]\stackrel{{\scriptstyle q}}{{\to}}W</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.E44.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\circ p=h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\circ p=h</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.E44.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a geometric quotient it is the coarse
moduli space for the stack <math id="S4.E44.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[V/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[V/G]</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S4.E44.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon W^{\prime}\to[V/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon W^{\prime}\to[V/G]</annotation></semantics></math> is
finite and representable
and its degree is the degree of the map <math id="S4.E44.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon V^{\prime}\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon V^{\prime}\to V</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we
may view <math id="S4.E44.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> as a finite map, but if we require that <math id="S4.E44.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\deg p)(\deg q)=\deg(q\circ p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\deg p)(\deg q)=\deg(q\circ p)</annotation></semantics></math> then we come to the surprising conclusion that <math id="S4.E44.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg q={1\over{e_{V}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg q={1\over{e_{V}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.E46" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E46.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.46</span></span><span id="S4.E46.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E46.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E46.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is possible for the coarse moduli scheme <math id="S4.E46.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of a smooth DM stack
<math id="S4.E46.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be smooth without <math id="S4.E46.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> being representable.
For example, if <math id="S4.E46.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathcal{X}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathcal{X}}=1</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S4.E46.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, since Theorem <a href="#S4.E31" title="Theorem 4.31. ‣ 4.2.1. Singularities of coarse moduli spaces ‣ 4.2. Coarse moduli spaces of Deligne-Mumford stacks ‣ 4. Stacks, moduli spaces and cohomology ‣ Equivariant
geometry and the cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a> implies that is
normal. However, it is not the case that there is an isomorphism of
<span id="S4.E46.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">integral</span> Chow rings <math id="S4.E46.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E46.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. For
example, if <math id="S4.E46.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> then its coarse moduli scheme
is
<math id="S4.E46.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M}_{1,1}={\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M}_{1,1}={\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="S4.E46.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1})={\mathbb{Z}}[t]/24t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1})={\mathbb{Z}}[t]/24t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> while
<math id="S4.E46.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}({\mathbb{P}}^{1})={\mathbb{Z}}[h]/h^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}({\mathbb{P}}^{1})={\mathbb{Z}}[h]/h^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, in the ring structure on <math id="S4.E46.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
induced by the ring structure on <math id="S4.E46.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1})</annotation></semantics></math> the identity
corresponds to <math id="S4.E46.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{1\over{2}}[{\mathbb{P}}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{1\over{2}}[{\mathbb{P}}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> because general elliptic curve
has an automorphism group of order 2. If the automorphism group of a general
point of <math id="S4.E46.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial then the identity will be <math id="S4.E46.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[M]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude with a discussion of the degree of a cycle on a complete DM
quotient stack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E47" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E47.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.47</span></span><span id="S4.E47.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E47.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E47.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.E47.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is complete <math id="S4.E47.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional DM stack with coarse moduli space <math id="S4.E47.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.E47.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in A_{0}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in A_{0}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> then we define <math id="S4.E47.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to be
the degree of its image under the isomorphism <math id="S4.E47.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{0}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}({\mathcal{X}})\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}\to A_{0}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S4.E47.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in A^{n}({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in A^{n}({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> we write <math id="S4.E47.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\mathcal{X}}c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\mathcal{X}}c</annotation></semantics></math> for the
degree of the image <math id="S4.E47.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\cap[{\mathcal{X}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\cap[{\mathcal{X}}]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.E47.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}(M)\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the degree of an integral cycle on a DM stack need not
be an integer. Our final example is an equivariant take on a
well known calculation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.E48" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.E48.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.48</span></span><span id="S4.E48.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.E48.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.E48.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will show that <math id="S4.E48.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}}\lambda_{1}=1/24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}}\lambda_{1}=1/24</annotation></semantics></math> using
equivariant methods.
Consider the action of <math id="S4.E48.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.E48.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with weights (4,6); i.e.
<math id="S4.E48.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(x,y)=(\lambda^{4}x,\lambda^{6}y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(x,y)=(\lambda^{4}x,\lambda^{6}y)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.E48.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X={\mathbb{A}}^{2}\smallsetminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X={\mathbb{A}}^{2}\smallsetminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an equivalence of categories
<math id="S4.E48.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}\to[X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,1}\to[X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}]</annotation></semantics></math> which associates to a family of elliptic
curves
<math id="S4.E48.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}T,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S4.E48.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S4.E48.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to T</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S4.E48.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of the zero section in the Hodge bundle
<math id="S4.E48.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}=\pi_{*}(\omega_{C/T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}=\pi_{*}(\omega_{C/T})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under this identification the Hodge bundle <math id="S4.E48.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponds to the line bundle <math id="S4.E48.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.E48.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, where the total space
of <math id="S4.E48.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.E48.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.E48.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.E48.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> with weight 1.
Now <math id="S4.E48.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}([X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}])={\mathbb{Z}}[t]/24t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}([X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}])={\mathbb{Z}}[t]/24t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.E48.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=c_{1}(L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=c_{1}(L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, our
problem
is to compute <math id="S4.E48.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{[X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}]}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{[X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}]}t</annotation></semantics></math>. To do this we must represent <math id="S4.E48.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>
(or more precisely <math id="S4.E48.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\cap[X]_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\cap[X]_{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>) in
terms of <math id="S4.E48.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant 0-cycles on <math id="S4.E48.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. There are two natural
choices,
the line<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
                  
                  
                  
                A line in <math id="footnote8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> determines an
element of <math id="footnote8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}^{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}^{{\mathbb{C}}^{*}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> because
we shift the degree by the dimension of the group when we define
equivariant Chow groups.</span></span></span>
<math id="S4.E48.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> or the line <math id="S4.E48.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The line <math id="S4.E48.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> is cut out by an
equation whose weight is <math id="S4.E48.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="S4.E48.p1.m30" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[x=0]=4t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x=0]=4t</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.E48.p1.m31" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t={1\over{4}}[x=0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t={1\over{4}}[x=0]</annotation></semantics></math>. A
point on this line has coordinates <math id="S4.E48.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,a)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.E48.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the stabilizer of such a point is <math id="S4.E48.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the degree of <math id="S4.E48.p1.m35" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[x=0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x=0]</annotation></semantics></math>
is 1/6 so <math id="S4.E48.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{[X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}]}t=1/4\times 1/6=1/24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{[X/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}]}t=1/4\times 1/6=1/24</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that if we had
chosen the line <math id="S4.E48.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> then we would see that <math id="S4.E48.p1.m38" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t={1\over{6}}[y=0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t={1\over{6}}[y=0]</annotation></semantics></math>
but the stabilizer at a point of the invariant subvariety <math id="S4.E48.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> has
order 4 - yielding 1/24 as well.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
